const CANVCTX = {"BTH-01":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether the PE firm should put $20M into a 400-room hotel on an army base — and the case turns not on 'is it profitable' but on 'does the money come back fast enough'.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt: the Army offers the land free and asks the client to decide whether to build. No hurdle is stated here; the 4-5 year target only appears at step 7.","matters":"Every box below feeds this one, but only two decide it — the $20M build and the $3.2M a year. The verdict is no, and it is a verdict about speed, not size."},"rev":{"concept":"Room-nights sold at the Army's price, and nothing else. No restaurant, no conferences, no amenities — though the case's own next-steps page says those should be looked at.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 4 as $7.8M, then cut at step 5 once the 80-room shortage is revealed. The interviewer withholds that shortage until after the first answer is given.","computes":"120,000 room-nights × $60 = $7.2M a year.","mental":"12 x 6 = 72, then count the zeros back: 4 in the volume, 1 in the price, so 5 in all. $7.2M. Do the digits first and the zeros last, every time.","matters":"Both its inputs are fixed by other people — a per diem sets the price, a training timetable sets the volume — so this is a box the client cannot grow."},"cost":{"concept":"Here the box holds two unlike things. $4M every year to run the hotel is the opex box below it; $20M once to build it is the capex box. The case never adds them.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Both given at step 6. The $4M is simply asserted; the $20M is a competitor's $50,000 a room applied to 400 rooms.","mental":"Do the small subtraction first: $7.2M - $4M = $3.2M. Then $3.2M - $20M is $20M - $3.2M with a minus in front: -$16.8M. Flip the order, then flip the sign.","check":"Ours: netted into one year, $7.2M − $4M − $20M = −$16.8M. That subtraction is the mistake the split guards against.","matters":"Adding them is the mistake. Kept apart, one gets divided into the other, and that division is the answer."},"vol":{"concept":"Room-nights the hotel can actually sell: three streams of soldiers, less the nights it is already full and turning people away. 129,000 is demand; 120,000 is what fits.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Built at step 4 from the class timetable, cut at step 5. The 3,000 temporary movers is 9,000 soldiers on a 3-year rotation — a division the casebook prints as finished.","computes":"(200×70×5) + (50×28×10) + (3,000×15) = 129,000, rounded to 130,000; less 80×4×30 = 9,600, rounded to 10,000 → 120,000.","mental":"In thousands: 200x70x5 = 70; 50x28x10 = 14; 3,000x15 = 45. So 70 + 14 + 45 = 129. The cut is 80x4x30, which is 80x120 = 9,600. Round both: 130 - 10 = 120.","check":"Unrounded, 129,000 − 9,600 = 119,400, and the casebook rounds both figures up before subtracting. It lands on 120,000 either way.","matters":"129,000 and 120,000 are both correct — before and after the capacity limit. Carrying the wrong one into revenue is the easiest mistake in this case."},"price":{"concept":"Not a price the client chose. The Army reimburses $75 a night and that must also buy the soldier breakfast and dinner, so the room has to sit below it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Handed over at step 4 — the client 'decides' on $60 and you assume it for the rest of the case. It is not derived from the pricing discussion that comes just before it.","mental":"$75 - $60 = $15, then ask what it buys: $15 across two meals is $7.50 each. Halving the leftover is the step that turns a subtraction into a verdict.","check":"Ours: $75 per diem − $60 room = $15 a day left for two meals. The case sets that test at step 3 and never runs it on $60.","matters":"A ceiling set by the payer, not by the market, so the usual fix — charge more — is shut. The recommendation page says exactly this."},"opex":{"concept":"The left term of the cost box: everything it takes to keep the hotel open for a year — staff, supplies, utilities, upkeep — handed over as a single lump.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given flat at step 6: 'assume total Op-ex of $4 million per year.' Never split into fixed and variable, so nothing tests whether it falls when rooms go unsold.","matters":"It is the only thing taken off revenue, so it alone sets the $3.2M that the $20M is later divided by."},"capex":{"concept":"The right term of the cost box: $20M paid once to build. The Army gave the land away, so this is the entire price of entry, spent before a single night is sold.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 6 from a benchmark, not a quote — the Hampton Inn paid $50,000 a room and the interviewer says to assume the same here.","computes":"400 rooms × $50,000 a room = $20M, all up front.","mental":"4 x 5 = 20, then count the zeros: two in 400, four in $50,000, six in all. $20M. Never grind 400 by 50,000 digit by digit when the digits are 4 and 5.","matters":"It sits outside the yearly profit, which is how the hotel can look healthy and still be a no. This is the number that has to be earned back."},"profit":{"concept":"One year of running the hotel: rooms in, running costs out. It says nothing about the $20M build, and that silence is the trap in this case.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 6, from revenue already cut by the capacity shortage.","computes":"$7.2M revenue − $4M operating cost = $3.2M a year.","mental":"$7.2M - $4M = $3.2M. Take the whole dollars first, 7 - 4 = 3, and carry the 0.2 along untouched. The decimal never needs to enter the subtraction.","matters":"$3.2M a year answers a question the client never asked. Alone it reads like a yes; the case turns on what it becomes once divided into $20M."},"pay":{"concept":"How many years of profit it takes to get the $20M back. The client set its pass mark in years rather than dollars: 4-5.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 7. The 4-5 year target is given only inside that same question, near the end — a candidate who never asks has nothing to compare against.","computes":"$20M ÷ $3.2M = 6.25 years. The casebook prints '6-7 years'; 6.25 is what the division gives.","mental":"32 x 6 = 192, so six-and-a-bit. The 8 left over is a quarter of 32, so 6.25 - and 'just over six' is already enough to fail a four-to-five-year test.","check":"Against the 4-5 year target the gap is at least 1.25 years. The casebook's rounding up to '6-7' widens a gap that was already there.","matters":"One division reverses the answer: the same hotel is a yes on profit and a no on payback."}},"BTH-02":{"decision":{"concept":"What to charge for a single-use surgical device — and only that. The client asks for the revenue-maximising price, not the profitable one, and the case answers exactly the question asked.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, narrowed twice: the United States only, and revenue rather than profit.","matters":"Because the question is revenue, the answer needs only two boxes — how many and at what price. Every cost box stays dark and the case is still complete."},"rev":{"concept":"The whole answer lives here. Revenue is tried at four prices and the winner is the one in the middle, not the one that sells to the most surgeons.","litAt":"step 3 · The curve","computes":"100,000 surgeries × 50% adoption × $600 = $30.0M — the highest of the four.","mental":"Take the percentage first: half of 100,000 is 50,000, then 5 x 6 = 30 with zeros 4 + 2 = 6, so $30M. Same route gives 75,000 x $300 = $22.5M and 10,000 x $1,000 = $10M.","check":"The other three: $0 at free, 75% × $300 = $22.5M, 10% × $1,000 = $10.0M. Seeing the arc is the point; one row proves nothing.","matters":"90% adoption at $0 earns nothing and 10% at $1,000 earns $10M — so 'more customers' and 'more money' point in different directions, which is the lesson."},"cost":{"concept":"Never supplied, and the dark box is the case's real limitation: the title page calls this a profitability case and no cost of any kind is ever given.","from":"Not in the case. Nothing is said about what the device costs to make, to sell or to ship, so the margin on any of the four prices is unknowable.","matters":"Without it $600 can only be called revenue-maximising. The case's own risks page concedes the price that maximises revenue may not maximise profit, then stops."},"vol":{"concept":"Units equal operations, because the device is thrown away after each one. Volume is then just the share of those operations whose surgeon agrees to the price.","litAt":"step 2 · Market size","from":"Given when asked: about 100,000 breast conservation surgeries a year in the US, usable in every one of them, one device per surgery.","computes":"100,000 surgeries × 1 device each = 100,000 units of headroom; at $600, 50% adopt → 50,000 sold.","mental":"One device per surgery means there is no multiplication at all - units are surgeries. Then half of 100,000 is 50,000. Halve before you reach for anything harder.","matters":"The 100,000 never changes across the four prices, so it is the adoption percentage alone that moves revenue — a rare case where market size is fixed and only mix matters."},"price":{"concept":"The one thing being decided. Four candidate prices are tested against surgeons' willingness to adopt, and the box holds the winner rather than a cost-plus calculation.","litAt":"step 3 · The curve","from":"The four price/adoption pairs appear only in the answer table. Exhibit 1 as printed carries no readable values, so the reader cannot see where $600 or 50% came from.","matters":"$600 is chosen because it earns most, not because it is affordable or defensible — no insurer, no competitor and no reimbursement cap is ever put against it."}},"BTH-03":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to open a 21st Burger Palace inside Midway's main terminal. The airport did the asking; the client will go ahead on one test only, whether the outlet turns a profit.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt; the guidance makes profitability the sole deciding factor and says management wants to move as fast as possible.","matters":"The answer is yes, on $70,000 a year — thin against $280,000 of fixed cost, which is why the breakeven question at the end carries as much weight as the profit."},"rev":{"concept":"A year of counter sales at the airport outlet. The case never writes this figure down: it jumps straight from customers to profit using a margin.","litAt":"step 2 · Sizing","from":"Never printed. The calculations go 500 customers → 20% margin → profit, skipping revenue entirely; the $14M market size at step 4 is the only revenue-shaped number given.","computes":"Ours: 500 customers/day × $10 × 350 days = $1.75M a year — one eighth of the $14M market the case does print.","mental":"500 x $10 = $5,000 a day. Then split the 350: $5,000 x 300 = $1.5M, $5,000 x 50 = $0.25M, so $1.75M. Break the awkward multiplier into round pieces.","matters":"Its absence is the shortcut being demonstrated: with a fixed 20% margin you can get from customers to profit without ever sizing revenue."},"cost":{"concept":"Two different shapes in one box: $280,000 a year that arrives whether or not anyone eats, and 80 cents out of every dollar taken at the till.","litAt":"step 3 · Profit","from":"Given at step 3. The $200,000 line is stated to include the initial construction of the restaurant, so the building of it never appears on its own.","computes":"Fixed: $200,000 rent and operating + $80,000 royalty to Midway = $280,000 a year. Variable: 80% of sales, leaving 20 cents of contribution in the dollar.","mental":"Work in thousands: 200 + 80 = 280, then put the zeros back, $280K. And 80% variable leaves 20 cents in the dollar, so contribution is simply a fifth of sales.","matters":"Splitting them is what makes the final question answerable — a breakeven share is only meaningful against the costs that do not move."},"vol":{"concept":"Customers through the door each day, built out of the airport itself: gates, flights, seats, how full the planes fly, and how few people stop to eat at all.","litAt":"step 2 · Sizing","from":"Built at step 2 from figures handed over one at a time as the candidate asks. The 2,000 employees are withheld until the passenger count is finished.","computes":"25 gates × 1 plane/hr × 16 hrs = 400 planes; × 140 passengers = 56,000/day; × 1/8 × 5% = 350. Employees: 2,000 × 1/8 × 60% = 150. Together 500 a day.","mental":"25 x 16 = 400. Then 400 x 140 is 4 x 14 = 56 with the zeros back, 56,000. An eighth of that is 7,000, and 5% of 7,000 is 350. Employees: 2,000 / 8 = 250, 60% of it 150.","check":"The 1/8 is assumption, not finding: 7 restaurants become 8, and the case simply declares the split equal and the total number of diners unchanged.","matters":"Everything downstream is 500 × something, so this box carries the case — and it is the box built almost entirely out of assumptions."},"price":{"concept":"What one customer spends in a visit, burger and fries together. An average ticket, not the price of anything on the menu board.","litAt":"step 3 · Profit","from":"Given at step 3 as a figure from other Burger Palaces. There is no data on the airport outlet itself, so the same $10 is assumed to hold there.","mental":"Turn the lump into a per-visit figure: 500 a day x 350 days is 175,000 visits, and $80,000 over that is about 46 cents. A 50-cent rise on the ticket pays the whole royalty.","check":"Ours: the next-steps page notes chains often charge more at airports to cover royalties. Nothing here tests a higher ticket, though $80,000 of royalty is what it would pay for.","matters":"The breakeven at step 4 also assumes every rival charges $10 — that is what lets a share of customers stand in for a share of revenue."},"opex":{"concept":"The fixed half of the cost box: rent, equipment, salaried staff and the airport's cut — all owed before a single burger is sold.","litAt":"step 3 · Profit","from":"Given at step 3 as two lines. The rent line quietly swallows the construction of the restaurant, so nothing at all is left to be paid once.","computes":"$200,000 rent and fixed operating + $80,000 annual royalty to Midway = $280,000 a year.","mental":"Work in thousands: 200 + 80 = 280, then put the three zeros back. Dropping to the smallest unit that keeps the digits is the whole trick with round money.","matters":"It is both what the $350,000 of contribution has to clear and, at step 4, the entire numerator of the breakeven share."},"capex":{"concept":"There is no build cost to see, and that is stated rather than forgotten: the construction of the restaurant is folded into the $200,000 annual rent line.","from":"Step 3, inside the parenthesis on the fixed-cost line: 'includes the initial construction of the new restaurant'.","matters":"With nothing paid once, there is nothing to earn back — which is why this case ends in a breakeven share rather than a payback in years."},"profit":{"concept":"A year of the airport outlet standing on its own: what 500 customers a day leave behind, less the costs the airport charges whether they come or not.","litAt":"step 3 · Profit","from":"Computed at step 3 by the margin route — the case never subtracts variable costs explicitly, it multiplies the $10 ticket by 20% instead.","computes":"$245,000 from passengers + $105,000 from employees = $350,000 contribution; − $280,000 fixed = $70,000 a year.","mental":"$245K + $105K: the 45 and the 5 make 50, so $350K. Then $350K - $280K by counting up - 280 to 300 is 20, 300 to 350 is 50, so $70K. Counting up beats borrowing.","matters":"$70,000 is the yes. It is also about 4% of sales, so any slip in the assumed 1/8 share takes it away — which is exactly why the breakeven gets asked."},"pay":{"concept":"Nothing is paid once, so there is nothing to pay back. The case swaps payback for a different margin of safety: how small a share still covers the fixed costs.","litAt":"step 4 · Breakeven","from":"Computed at step 4, as an optional extension offered only to candidates who first flagged the 1/8 share as a risk.","computes":"Market size (2,800 + 1,200 diners) × $10 × 350 days = $14M; then $280,000 ÷ ($14M × 20%) = 10%.","mental":"4,000 x $10 = $40,000 a day; x 350 is 4 x 35 = 140 with the zeros back, $14M. Then $280K against $2.8M is the same digits ten apart, so 10%.","check":"12.5% expected against 10% needed: a cushion of 2.5 points, one fifth of the volume — the same thinness that $70,000 on $1.75M of sales shows.","matters":"It turns the answer from a number into a margin, and a narrow one: lose a fifth of the expected customers and the outlet merely breaks even."}},"BTH-04":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to spend $300M over two years on the final FDA trial. The case turns on how fast that $300M comes back, not on whether the vaccine is a good business.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, together with a 95% chance of approval that the casebook's own arithmetic then never uses.","check":"Ours: at 95% the expected three-year profit is 0.95 × $756M ≈ $718M. Still more than twice the $300M, so the answer holds — but the case never runs the test.","mental":"95% means 'take off a twentieth'. A twentieth of $756M is about $38M, so $718M. Subtracting 5% is always easier than multiplying by 0.95.","matters":"The answer is yes, and comfortably. The genuinely hard work is seeing what the business is worth after year five, not whether to fund the trial."},"rev":{"concept":"Three years of emptying a queue at $360M a year, then a much smaller stream from the babies born each year. Two businesses stacked on top of each other, not one.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Built at step 5; neither input is given. Volume comes from Exhibit 1 plus a population assumption released only on request, price from the Exhibit 2 demand curve.","computes":"12M injections × $30 = $360M a year for three years, then 3M × $30 = $90M a year from year six.","mental":"12 × 3 = 36, then count the zeros back on: six for the M and one for the 30, giving $360M. Do the digits first and the zeros last, every time.","matters":"Three quarters of the revenue is a stock being drawn down, not a flow. Valuing the vaccine at $360M a year is exactly the mistake the case is built to catch."},"cost":{"concept":"Two unlike things in one box. $9 of ingredients, delivery and selling on every injection, and one $300M trial paid before any injection exists. The case keeps them apart.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"The $300M is given in the prompt; the three per-shot components are released only if the candidate asks for them at step 4.","computes":"$1.50 distribution + $2.50 production + $5.00 SG&A = $9.00 an injection, 30% of the $30 price.","mental":"Pair the numbers that make a round one: 1.50 + 2.50 is a clean $4, then + $5 = $9. Hunting for the friendly pair first beats adding left to right.","matters":"At $9 against $30 the margin is 70% and no cost decision is in play. All the leverage sits in the $300M, which is why it is never mixed into the per-shot figure."},"vol":{"concept":"Injections — and the vaccine is a single shot, so each person is a customer exactly once. That is why the queue empties and never refills at the same rate.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit 1","from":"Built at step 4 from assumptions the interviewer supplies only on request: 300M people spread evenly over ages 0–75, and three years to work through the existing backlog.","computes":"300M ÷ 75 = 4M an age year; × 18 = 72M aged 0–18; × 50% uninfected = 36M; × 75% willing to pay = 27M; ÷ 3 years = 9M, plus 4M births × 75% = 3M → 12M a year.","mental":"75 × 4 = 300, so 4M an age year. Then halving and taking three quarters is just three eighths: 72M ÷ 8 = 9M, × 3 = 27M. Chain fractions rather than stepping twice.","matters":"9M of the 12M vanishes after year three. Getting that split right is the whole case — the interviewer guidance names it as the key insight before the case begins."},"price":{"concept":"Read off a demand curve, not calculated. $30 is where the curve bends: below it buyers barely respond to price, above it they leave quickly.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 2","from":"Exhibit 2, the inflection point. The casebook argues for it rather than deriving it, and adds that at $30 only 75% of parents will buy at all.","check":"Ours: $25 would hold slightly more of the market but give up $5 on all 27M backlog shots — $135M over the three years. The case never runs that comparison.","mental":"$5 × 27M: halve and multiply by ten — 27 halved is 13.5, times 10 is 135, so $135M. Multiplying by 5 is always halve-then-times-ten.","matters":"The 75% at $30 is what turns 36M treatable people into 27M paying ones. Here price does not merely set revenue per shot; it sets volume as well."},"opex":{"concept":"What each additional injection costs to make and sell. It scales with volume, so it never pressures the decision — the one-off $300M does.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 2","from":"Given only on request at step 4, as three separate lines. Note that SG&A at $5.00 is larger than production and distribution put together.","computes":"$30 − $9 = $21 of contribution a shot, a 70% margin.","mental":"30 − 9 is 30 − 10 + 1 = 21. Round the number you are subtracting up to a ten, then hand the difference back.","matters":"$21 a shot is what every later figure runs on — the $756M, the $63M annuity and the competitor's ten-year breakeven all reduce to this one number."},"capex":{"concept":"The phase-three trial. The case says to treat it as covering everything else too, plant set-up included, so this single figure is the entire cost of entry.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given in the prompt; step 4 adds that it may be expensed evenly over the two years of testing and that it already includes plant set-up costs.","check":"Ours: phases 1 and 2 cost $200M and are already spent. The case is explicit that they are sunk for our decision but must be counted in a competitor's.","mental":"The sunk-cost test is one question: would the number change if we said no today? $200M is gone either way, so it never enters. $300M is not yet spent, so it does.","matters":"It is the only thing standing against the profit, and the case's entire verdict is the ratio of one to the other."},"profit":{"concept":"Contribution, not accounting profit — revenue less the $9 a shot, with nothing taken off for the trial. The trial is compared against it afterwards, not deducted from it.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 2","from":"Computed at step 5 from the contribution margin. The casebook's own summary prints $756M for the first three years and $63M a year thereafter.","computes":"12M × $21 = $252M a year, so $756M across three years; then 3M × $21 = $63M a year from year six onwards.","mental":"12 × 21 = 12 × 20 + 12 = 252. Split the awkward factor into a round part and a remainder — never try to multiply by 21 directly.","matters":"$63M is what the client is actually buying; the $252M years are a one-off draw-down. Capitalising $252M as if it continued overvalues the vaccine about fourfold."},"pay":{"concept":"How long the backlog years take to return the $300M — measured from the first sale, not from the day the money is spent.","litAt":"step 5 · The table","from":"Computed at step 5. The casebook prints '~1.25 years' twice without ever showing the division that produced it.","computes":"$300M ÷ $252M = 1.19 years. One way to reach the printed 1.25 is $300M ÷ $240M, but the case's own figures give 1.19.","check":"Ours: a rival entering at year six shares 3M shots. ($30 − $9) × 1.5M = $31.5M a year, so $300M ÷ $31.5M ≈ 10 years — the casebook's own reason nobody comes.","mental":"252 is just over 250 and 300 ÷ 250 = 1.2, so a shade under: 1.19. For the rival, 300 ÷ 31.5 is 300 ÷ 30 nudged down, about 10. Round the divisor, then lean the answer.","matters":"A trial repaid inside fifteen months of selling is an easy yes at any hurdle rate — which is why the casebook can assert a high positive NPV without computing one."}},"BTH-05":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to accept one fixed bundle of price changes across five products. It is all of them or none — the case forbids picking the good ones and dropping the bad.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"The prompt only says the client wants to 'do better in the market'; the real question — beat a 1% revenue rise — is not stated until step 4.","matters":"Because the changes move together, the answer is a single sum, not five decisions. Judging any one product on its own gets the case wrong."},"rev":{"concept":"The only box the client is judged on. With volumes held still, revenue moves purely with price, so this box is the case's entire scoreboard.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"$3 billion last year, given at step 2 only if the candidate asks. The product split behind it is in Exhibit 1, which is an image — no product-level figures are in the text.","computes":"$3.000B + $39M = $3.039B after the change. The case prints both ends; the per-product columns that bridge them cannot be read.","mental":"Put both in one unit before adding: $3B is 3,000M, so 3,000 + 39 = 3,039M. Mixing billions and millions inside a single sum is how a factor of a thousand goes missing.","matters":"$39M on $3B is the whole recommendation. It clears the client's bar by $9M, which is a thin margin to rest a five-product price rise on."},"cost":{"concept":"Closed off before it opens. The client is simply told there are no cost savings and no new products to add, so two-thirds of the profitability tree is removed by assertion.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given as a flat statement at step 2, with no evidence: 'There are no opportunities in cost savings or by adding new products to the mix.'","matters":"That one sentence is what forces the case into price. It is also what makes the answer fragile — the brainstorm then admits price rises may bring costs of their own."},"vol":{"concept":"Not a number but an assumption: volumes stay exactly where they are at the new prices. Nobody buys less, in any of the five lines.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit","from":"Stated as the premise of Exhibit 1 — the research says volumes 'will remain unchanged' if all the prices move at once. It is an input, not a finding.","matters":"The exhibit is titled 'Price Elasticity' while assuming demand does not respond to price at all. That assumption, not the arithmetic, is what earns the $39M."},"price":{"concept":"Five different price moves, up on some lines and down on others, which the canvas can only hold as a blended figure. No single product's change is visible here.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit","from":"The per-product percentages are in Exhibit 1, printed as an image; none of them survive in the text, so the blend cannot be rebuilt from the individual moves.","computes":"Ours: $39M ÷ $3B = about +1.3% across the book. The case never states a blended figure.","mental":"Anchor on 1%: 1% of $3B is $30M. $39M is that plus a third of it again, so 1% plus 0.3%, about +1.3%. Never divide 39 by 3,000 longhand.","matters":"A 1.3% average tells you the big revenue lines decided this, not the big percentage moves — a large rise on a small product cannot shift a $3B total."},"profit":{"concept":"Never computed, and the box is worth noticing empty: the client's target is set in revenue and the decision is taken on revenue, with the bottom line never reached.","from":"Not in the case. With costs ruled out at step 2, no cost figure exists to subtract, so the $39M in this box is a revenue gain wearing a profit box's label.","mental":"1% is dividing by 100: $3B becomes $30M. Then $39M - $30M = $9M. Read the cushion as a fraction too - $9M on $39M is under a quarter of the gain.","check":"$3B × 1% = $30M target; $39M − $30M = $9M clear. Both sides of that test are revenue.","matters":"The case's own brainstorm says advertising to explain the price rise may eat into the $39M — $9M of headroom does not survive much of that, and it is never quantified."}},"BTH-07":{"decision":{"concept":"Not 'should we grow in China' but 'should we spend $500M standing up a second, cheaper brand'. That question only appears at step 5, after two steps of pure discussion.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"The prompt asks how to profitably grow share. The $500M choice arrives at step 5; the four-year breakeven requirement is released only on request at step 6.","matters":"The case answers it with a breakeven share and calls that a go. Breakeven is where $500M has returned exactly $500M — it recommends striving for a zero return."},"rev":{"concept":"$50K a truck times however many are sold. The case never once computes it — the whole argument runs on one truck's margin, so this box stays an expression.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Price given on request at step 6; the unit count arrives at step 7 as a requirement, not a forecast. No revenue line is printed anywhere in the case.","mental":"50 x 50 = 2,500; thousands times thousands make millions, so $2.5B of sales. The $125M of margin is a twentieth of it, because $2.5K is a twentieth of $50K.","check":"Ours: 50,000 × $50K = $2.5B of sales a year to produce $125M of margin. The case never states the scale of the business it is recommending building.","matters":"Its absence is defensible — at a 5% margin, per-truck is the right unit — but nobody working the case ever sees how big this business would have to be."},"cost":{"concept":"Two unlike things: $47.5K inside every truck ($37.5K to build it, $10K to sell and administer it), and $500M paid once for plant, supply base and dealers.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given on request, the $500M at step 5 and the per-truck costs at step 6. COGS arrives as a percentage — 75% of price — not as a dollar figure.","mental":"No sum, just a direction: move cost from the per-truck side to the $500M side and the divisor shrinks while the numerator grows. The share needed rises from both ends at once.","check":"Ours: the $10K of SG&A is treated as entirely variable. If any of it is really fixed it belongs beside the $500M, and the share the client needs goes up.","matters":"Kept apart, the per-truck cost sets the margin and the $500M gets divided by it. That division is the only quantitative work the case does."},"vol":{"concept":"Not trucks the case expects to sell — trucks that must be sold. Everything else is fixed, so volume is solved backwards out of the $500M.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 7. The four-year window is the client's own requirement, given at step 6; a flat market 'for ease of calculation' lets one year stand for all four.","computes":"$500M ÷ $2.5K = 200,000 trucks; 200,000 ÷ 4 years = 50,000 a year.","mental":"Factor the zeros out: 500 divided by 2.5 is 200, and millions over thousands leaves thousands, so 200,000. Dividing by 2.5 is quicker as times four, divide by ten.","check":"Ours: volume is flat across all four years — no ramp-up for a brand that does not yet exist, and year-four margin counted the same as year-one margin.","matters":"50,000 a year from a standing start is the claim the recommendation rests on, and a flat market means every one must be taken off an incumbent."},"price":{"concept":"$50K, set to match the Chinese rivals rather than derived from anything. The point of the new brand is a deliberately worse truck at their price.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given on request at step 6. Step 5 says only that Ndamukong would be priced 'similarly to the competition'; $50K makes that concrete without arguing for it.","mental":"Per year of life, divide: 50 over 4 is 12.5. The rival is 200 over 20, which is halving then dropping a zero, so 10. The dearer truck is the cheaper one to own.","check":"Ours: $50K over 4 years of life is $12.5K a year; Megatron's truck at 4× the price over 20 years is $10K. On the payback logic the case cites, the local truck is dearer.","matters":"Everything hangs off it — COGS is defined as 75% of it. The case never tests a different price, though price is the one lever the new brand actually chose."},"opex":{"concept":"What one truck costs to make and sell: $37.5K of build cost plus $10K of selling and admin. All of it treated as moving with every unit, none of it fixed.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given on request at step 6. COGS is stated as a share of price, so it is a ratio the case assumes rather than a cost anyone has estimated.","computes":"75% × $50K = $37.5K, plus $10K of SG&A = $47.5K a truck.","mental":"75% is three quarters, so take a quarter of $50K, which is $12.5K, and treble it: $37.5K. Quarter-and-treble beats multiplying by 0.75 every time.","matters":"It leaves $2.5K, and no depreciation, launch marketing, working capital or corporate overhead is ever added to it. The thinnest number in the case sits here."},"capex":{"concept":"$500M once for a factory, a supply base and a dealer network — the whole price of entry for a brand that does not exist yet, spent before a single truck is sold.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 5 as a simplification: 'to simplify, the required investment would consist of...'. No breakdown, no quote, no phasing over the four years.","matters":"It is the numerator of the entire case. Divided by $2.5K it becomes 200,000 trucks, and everything after that is just rearranging that one figure."},"profit":{"concept":"Not company profit — what one truck leaves behind once it is built and sold. The case's own term is variable margin, and $2.5K on a $50K truck is 5% of price.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 6 from the price, the COGS percentage and the SG&A, all three released on request in the same breath.","computes":"$50K − $37.5K − $10K = $2.5K a truck.","mental":"Subtract the cost total, not one piece at a time: $50K less $47.5K is $2.5K. And $2.5K in $50K is a twentieth, which is 5%. Every twentieth you meet is 5%.","check":"Ours: that is 5% of price, never stated. Move COGS from 75% to 76% and the margin falls to $2K and the required share rises from 12.5% to 15.6%.","matters":"Every figure downstream is $500M divided by this. A margin this thin should have been stress-tested and no sensitivity is run on it anywhere."},"pay":{"concept":"Payback expressed as a market share rather than as years — the years are fixed at four, so the question becomes what slice of China you must hold to get $500M back inside them.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 7. The 400,000-unit Chinese market is 500K global × 80%, both given at step 6; the four-year window is the client's own stated requirement.","computes":"$500M ÷ $2.5K = 200,000 trucks; ÷ 4 years = 50,000; 50,000 ÷ 400,000 = 12.5% share.","mental":"Cancel the zeros first: 50,000 over 400,000 is 5 over 40, one eighth. An eighth is 12.5%, worth memorising alongside a half, a third, a quarter and a fifth.","check":"Domestic firms hold 90%, so every foreign maker in China together sells about 40,000 trucks a year. The plan needs 50,000 from one brand that does not exist.","matters":"12.5% is a requirement, not a forecast, and the case treats it as an answer. Its own next step — test whether 12.5% is reachable — comes after the recommendation."}},"BTH-09":{"decision":{"concept":"A feeling rather than a symptom: BB 'is not doing as well as it could' against a rival selling the same number of the same lenses. The first job is turning that into profitability.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, which contains no number at all. Four background facts — US only, 3% growth, 10 million lenses each, high barriers to entry — arrive only if you ask.","matters":"Equal volume, equal share, equal cost, equal list price. Once those are closed, the only thing left that can differ is the proportions — who you sell through — and the case is that deduction."},"rev":{"concept":"Revenue for one box of six lenses, and it depends entirely on who is buying. The same lens fetches three different sums because three kinds of buyer have three kinds of power.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 2's top row at step 5. BB and its rival charge the same in each channel, established at step 2 — so no figure here is a difference between the two companies.","computes":"A doctor's office pays $28.00 against a big-box discounter's $16.50: 1.7 times as much for an identical box.","mental":"Bracket before dividing: double 16.5 to get 33, which overshoots 28, so the answer is under 2. Add half of 16.5 and you are at 24.75, so it is about 1.7.","matters":"The price gap is modest. Only when the cost of serving each buyer is set beside it do the channels separate, which is why this row on its own proves nothing."},"cost":{"concept":"Three rows added together: the lens itself at $8 everywhere, what it costs to sell and deliver, and a $2 slice of head office. Only the middle one varies with the customer.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Exhibit 2 prints the rows at step 5; the totals are ours. The casebook subtracts them one at a time and never names a cost per box.","computes":"Big box $8 + $5 + $2 = $15. Doctor's office $8 + $2 + $2 = $12. Optical retailer $8 + $3.50 + $2 = $13.50.","mental":"Factor out what is common before adding: $8 and $2 appear in every column, so carry $10 and add only the selling line — $5, $2, $3.50.","matters":"The buyer who pays least also costs most to serve. Those two move in opposite directions, and that is what turns a 1.7-to-1 price gap into a 10-to-1 profit gap."},"vol":{"concept":"Ten million lenses, six to a box. The case states volume in lenses and money in boxes and never converts between them, so you have to do it before the exhibits can be joined.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 1","from":"Ours. The 10 million lenses sits in the interviewer's background pack, released only on request; 'six lenses to a box' appears only in Exhibit 2's header.","computes":"10M ÷ 6 ≈ 1.67M boxes a year. At BB's 8 / 63 / 29 mix that is 133k big box, 1.05M doctors, 483k optical.","check":"The reps: 5 selling hours ÷ 0.5 hours a call = 10 calls a day, × 20 days = 200 a month, less the 100 obligatory calls = 100 free.","mental":"Dividing by 6 is halving then thirding: 10M → 5M → 1.67M. And dividing by 0.5 doubles — 5 hours of calls is 10 calls, never 2.5. A divisor under one always enlarges.","matters":"Two quantities, both fixed by somebody else: 1.67M boxes and 200 calls. Neither can grow, so the only moves left are which channel gets the boxes and which accounts get the calls."},"price":{"concept":"Not a price BB sets case by case: it is what each kind of buyer can extract. Walmart employs people whose whole job is to negotiate it down; a single eye doctor does not.","litAt":"step 3 · Narrowing","from":"Read off Exhibit 2 at step 5. Step 2 has already ruled price out as a difference between the rivals — it is a difference between their customers.","matters":"Both firms charge the same list prices, so the only way price can explain a profit gap is through the mix of who buys. That is the case's entire argument in one line."},"opex":{"concept":"Sale and distribution: the reps, the freight, the paperwork each kind of customer demands. It is the only row in the table that moves for reasons that are not the product.","litAt":"step 5 · Exhibit 2","from":"Read off Exhibit 2 at step 5. The $2 'other fixed costs' beside it is identical in all three columns, which shows it was divided evenly rather than measured.","check":"That even $2 does not actually move when a box shifts channel. Strip it out and the margins are 21%, 64% and 48% rather than 9%, 57% and 39% — the ranking survives.","mental":"Anchor on a tenth: 10% of $16.50 is $1.65, and $3.50 is a shade over double that, so about 21%. Doubling a tenth is the fastest route to a fifth.","matters":"A big-box discounter costs two and a half times as much to serve as a doctor while paying less. This row, not the price row, is what makes that channel nearly worthless."},"profit":{"concept":"Profit on one box in each channel, and the hinge of the case. A doctor's office earns more than ten times what a big-box discounter earns on the identical product.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 5 from Exhibit 2. All three profits and all three margins check out exactly as the casebook prints them.","computes":"$16.50 − $8 − $5 − $2 = $1.50 (9%). $28 − $8 − $2 − $2 = $16.00 (57%). $22 − $8 − $3.50 − $2 = $8.50 (39%).","check":"Ours: BB's mix earns about $21.1M a year, the rival's mix on the same volume about $22.75M. That $1.6M gap is the size of the problem, and the case never computes it.","mental":"Halve to judge a margin: half of 28 is 14, and $16 is a little more, so just over 57%. For the sizing, 1.05M × $16 is 16 × 1.05 — 16 plus 5% of 16, so $16.8M.","matters":"BB sends 63% of its lenses to doctors against the rival's 75%. Twelve points of mix, at $16 a box against $8.50, is the whole difference between two identical companies."}},"BTH-11":{"decision":{"concept":"Why the profit on coal-generated electricity is falling — a diagnosis, not a choice. Nothing is being bought, built or priced; the case only has to name a cause.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt: 10 coal plants, coal from both its own mines and third parties, profitability 'of late' in decline. No period, no size, no figures.","matters":"The case reaches 'costs went up' by shutting the revenue side rather than by measuring costs — the conclusion is a structure, not a finding, and the reader should see which one he is being given."},"rev":{"concept":"Present but closed. Electricity use is deregulated, so the market sets the price and the client takes it; there is no revenue lever to pull, only a revenue number to accept.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given as description at step 2, never as a figure. No revenue, no price and no volume history appears anywhere in the case.","matters":"Ruling this side out is what makes the rest of the case about cost. But a falling market price would explain a falling profit just as well, and the case never tests it."},"cost":{"concept":"Everything the case has left, and it is split by the journey the coal makes rather than by fixed and variable: acquiring it, burning it, sending the power out.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Structured at step 3 as a three-part supply chain. Every item under it is a candidate cause offered in words — transport, coal quality, old plant, union labour, new environmental rules.","matters":"A value chain is being used as a checklist for where a rise could have happened, not as a measurement of where it did. It generates hypotheses; it cannot confirm one."},"vol":{"concept":"Households and businesses buying power — over a million of them — in a market where supply already meets all demand, so selling more is not available.","from":"Given at step 2 when asked: over 1M customers, a fragmented market growing about 3% a year, one price for all of them.","matters":"3% growth with supply already meeting demand is the case's own reason for dropping the revenue side — the only quantified step in the whole argument, and it is one line."},"price":{"concept":"Set outside the company by competitive forces, fixed once for the whole year, and the same for every customer. The client is a price taker in a box normally full of choices.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 2 as three simplifications, with no figure attached. Price discrimination appears in the framework and is immediately assumed away.","matters":"One price a year means the client cannot respond to a cost rise within the year — which is exactly how a cost increase turns straight into a profit decline."},"opex":{"concept":"Two running costs sit here: coal, which is the raw material, and the lease on the transmission wires, which the client rents because the grid is government controlled.","litAt":"step 3 · Value chain","from":"Given at step 2 as categories only. Neither the coal price nor the lease is ever given a number, at any point in the case.","matters":"The 30% discount on its own coal looks like the saving that fixes this box, and it is not one: the same coal could be sold at market price, so burning it costs the market price either way."},"capex":{"concept":"The assets already owned — 10 coal plants and the mines feeding them. Nothing is being built, so the question is whether to keep what is there, not what to spend.","litAt":"step 5 · Utilisation","from":"The 10 plants come from the prompt; the mines from the same line. No build cost, book value or sale value is ever given for either.","mental":"80 against 77 is 3 points, but as a share it is 3/77, near 4%. The CEO's 90 is 10 points on 80, which is an eighth more, or +12.5%. Points and percent are not the same thing.","check":"Utilisation runs at 80% against an industry average of 77%, and the CEO wants 90%. The case values those 10 points in neither direction.","matters":"The case keeps the mine on supplier power and diversification — real arguments, but unpriced ones, standing where the 30% saving was just shown to be illusory."},"profit":{"concept":"The falling thing the whole case is about, and never once a number. The reader who wonders how far it fell is right to: the case does not say.","from":"Not in the case. No profit, revenue or cost figure appears at any step, which is unusual for a profitability case and is worth noticing.","matters":"With no size to the decline, nothing can be prioritised — the next steps ask for the top five cost savings, which is the measurement the case should have begun with."}},"BTH-12":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to roll the split-role pilot out to televisions, laptops and the rest — judged, in the end, entirely on what happened to phone profit in the average store.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The interviewer adds that the client wants a broad look beyond profit — morale, customer experience, brand — and none of those is ever measured.","matters":"The verdict is no, on one number that fell. The next steps then say redesign the incentive and re-measure, which is a different verdict, and the case never reconciles them."},"rev":{"concept":"Money taken on phone sales — and where the puzzle starts: total revenue rose sharply while revenue in the average store fell.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3: $36M in 2016, $44M in 2017. The per-store line is the exhibit's own division, printed for you.","computes":"$36M ÷ 120 stores = $300K; $44M ÷ 160 stores = $275K, down about 8%.","mental":"Cancel zeros: 36 over 120 is 0.3, 44 over 160 is 0.275. Losing 25 from 300 is a twelfth, about 8%, while the totals rose 8 on 36, a bit over 22%. Two directions, one business.","check":"Totals rose 22% while the per-store average fell 8%. The store count grew by a third for reasons the prompt says predate the pilot, and the case never separates the two.","matters":"The fall per store is the whole evidence against the pilot. Read as totals, the same exhibit says revenue and profit both went up."},"cost":{"concept":"Only one cost ever appears: what Elena's pays for a handset — $700, $400, $200 against prices of $1,000, $500, $250. Staff cost, the thing the pilot changed, never does.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 2 at step 4, as a COGS column beside the price column. The same three costs serve both years — no year-on-year change is given or implied.","matters":"With cost per handset fixed, every movement in profit here is mix and nothing else. That is what makes the case solvable, and also what makes it incomplete."},"vol":{"concept":"Handsets out of the door, nearly doubled: 54,000 to 104,000. The pilot plainly worked at selling more phones, which is what makes the case worth doing.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3. Exhibit 2 splits the same totals by model and the two reconcile exactly — a check the case never asks anyone to make.","computes":"54,000 ÷ 120 = 450 a store; 104,000 ÷ 160 = 650 a store, up 44%.","mental":"A tenth of 120 is 12, so 54 is 4.5 tenths, 450. 104 over 160 is 6.5 tenths, 650. The rise is 200 on 450, which is under half, so 44% and not 'nearly 50'.","check":"Per store, budget went 100 → 400 and deluxe 200 → 100. The recommendation quotes those as '4x' and '−50%' but calls them totals; in totals they are 5.3× and −33%.","matters":"Units rising while revenue falls is the signal that mix moved. Taken alone, this box says the pilot was a success."},"price":{"concept":"Three fixed price points for three phones, unchanged across both years: $1,000 deluxe, $500 midrange, $250 budget. Nobody discounted — customers simply bought differently.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 2 at step 4, revealed only once the candidate asks what could explain the change in mix.","matters":"Because prices held still, the entire revenue story is which of the three got sold. Four budget phones at $250 do not replace one deluxe at $1,000."},"opex":{"concept":"The pilot split one job into two, and what those people cost never appears — not in either year, not under either model. This is the empty box that should be loudest.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Never given and never asked for. The case's own framework lists cost under profitability; the only cost supplied is the handset, which the pilot could not change.","matters":"Every profit figure here is gross — price minus handset. A staffing experiment is being judged by a measure that cannot detect staffing, in either direction."},"profit":{"concept":"Gross profit on phones in the average store: what the handsets sold there made before anyone is paid. It fell while volume rose, and that fall is the recommendation.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 5 from Exhibit 2, two ways that agree — per-store units × margin, or total profit ÷ store count.","computes":"2016: $60K + $15K + $5K = $80K. 2017: $30K + $15K + $20K = $65K a store.","mental":"Each line is units x margin, and the margins are round: $300, $100, $50. Add in thousands: 60 + 15 + 5 = 80 against 30 + 15 + 20 = 65. Down $15K on $80K, near a fifth.","check":"In totals, phone profit rose from $9.6M to $10.4M, up 8%. Both figures are printed in the case's own solution and neither reaches the recommendation.","matters":"$80K → $65K is the whole case against the pilot. Whether it was the pilot or the 40 stores opened that year is never separated, and there is no control group."}},"BTH-13":{"decision":{"concept":"Not whether to enter — Finance Co has already decided that. Which of two doors: the employer-run 401k market, or the accounts people open and own themselves.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, which also half-answers it: both markets hold ~$3T and earn the same margin per dollar, so only growth is left to decide on.","matters":"Because size and margin are set equal by assertion, every branch except demographics is dead. The answer is which age bands hold which account, and which way the country is ageing."},"rev":{"concept":"Assets under administration, not sales. Finance Co earns a fee on every dollar of retirement savings it looks after, so the size of the pile is the revenue line.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Asserted in the prompt as '~$3 trillion' each. Exhibits 1–3 let you rebuild it yourself at steps 4 and 5: $3,000B for DC, $2,996B for IRA.","computes":"DC: 140×$1.5K + 45×$18K + 40×$27K + 30×$23K + 20×$10.5K + 20×$0 = $3,000B. IRA, the same way across Exhibit 3 = $2,996B.","mental":"Millions × thousands = billions, so drop every zero and multiply the digits: 45 × 18 = 810, write $810B. Do all six, then add — the total must land on $3,000.","check":"Both totals close on the prompt's $3T, which is a free audit of your own reading of two charts. The case never asks anyone to run it.","matters":"Equal size is what kills size as a tie-breaker. It is also why the five-year totals can be compared directly: both markets start from the same base."},"cost":{"concept":"Money leaving DC plans, not money spent running them. Lump sums taken out at job change or retirement — the outflow that feeds the other market.","litAt":"step 7 · Exhibit 5","from":"Given on Exhibit 5, and on a different clock from everything before it: one year, where Exhibits 1–4 measure a stock at two dates five years apart.","computes":"$200B × 75% = $150B rolled into IRAs. The other 25% is cashed out or moved to another employer's DC plan, so it never reaches an IRA.","mental":"75% is three quarters. A quarter of 200 is 50, so three of them is 150 — and the 50 left over is already the answer to the next question.","check":"$200B a year is $1,000B over five, against a demographic model that grows DC by $193.5B. The two halves of the case cannot both be right.","matters":"It is the only figure in the case showing money actually moving. The casebook calls rollovers the key growth factor and, one paragraph later, removes this exhibit from the argument."},"vol":{"concept":"Not customers — the whole US population in six age bands, because the balances you multiply by are averages across everybody, savers and non-savers alike.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3; the five-year band growth rates arrive with Exhibit 4 at step 6. That the bars are everybody is never stated — you confirm it by adding.","computes":"140+45+40+30+20+20 = 295M. Changes: +7, −2.25, +2, +6, +3, +1 = +16.75M, giving 311.75M.","mental":"Add the five small bars first — 45+40+30+20+20 = 155 — then put the 140 on top. Leaving the biggest number until last turns five awkward sums into one easy one.","check":"The bands are not the same width. 140M spread over 35 years of age is 4M a year; 40M over the ten years of 45–54 is also 4M. The tall bar is width, not youth.","matters":"Which bands grow decides the case: 55–64 grows 20% and 65–74 grows 15%, and those are exactly the bands where IRA balances are large and DC balances have collapsed."},"price":{"concept":"Nothing is charged here. The per-head slot holds the average balance a person of that age keeps in each account — the multiplier that turns people into a market.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Exhibits 2 and 3, at steps 4 and 5. Both are averages across a whole age band including people holding nothing, which is why the under-35 figures are so small.","check":"$27K DC against $7.5K IRA at 45–54, then $23K against $28K at 55–64 — the crossover sits between those two bands. The 75+ DC bar is blank.","mental":"To prove that blank bar is a hard zero, not missing data: the five printed DC bars already total the $3,000B the prompt promised, so anything more would break it.","matters":"The two curves are one person's life, not two markets. DC fills while you work and empties into an IRA when you stop, and transfer runs that way only."},"opex":{"concept":"Not running costs. The mirror of the DC outflow: money arriving in IRAs over a year, two thirds of it rolled over from DC plans and the rest new saving.","litAt":"step 7 · Exhibit 5","from":"Given on Exhibit 5. The panel is labelled inflows and shows only inflows — no retiree withdrawals appear anywhere in the case, though the prompt says they happen.","computes":"$150B of rollovers out of $225B of total inflow = 66.7%, so new contributions are the remaining $75B.","mental":"150 out of 225: both divide by 75, so it is exactly 2/3. Spotting the common factor beats long division, and two thirds is a split you can see on the bar.","matters":"It sizes the pipe the whole case rests on: $150B a year travels from DC to IRA and nothing travels back. The growth story has a mechanism, not just a correlation."},"capex":{"concept":"Empty, and the emptiness is the finding. Finance Co is told to enter a $3 trillion market and the cost of entering it is never named, estimated or asked about.","from":"Nowhere in seven pages. Finance Co's financial position appears in the risk list and its sales capability in the next steps — both after the recommendation is made.","matters":"The case measures the prize to the tenth of a trillion and never prices the ticket. A market-entry answer with no entry cost is a preference, not a decision."},"profit":{"concept":"One sentence in the prompt sets margin per dollar equal in both markets, so profit stops being a variable and becomes a constant you are handed.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Asserted in the prompt — 'about the same amount of margin per dollar' — and never supported by a single number anywhere in the case.","matters":"It is the permission slip for everything after it. If a dollar earns the same either way, the market holding more dollars in five years simply wins, and strategy becomes arithmetic."},"pay":{"concept":"No payback here — the box holds the five-year finish line: the two market sizes the whole case exists to compare.","litAt":"step 6 · Exhibit 4","from":"Computed at step 6 from Exhibit 4's growth rates. The casebook prints both totals at step 8 and shows no line of working for either.","computes":"Growth only: IRA 7(0.4) − 2.25(4) + 2(7.5) + 6(28) + 3(51) + 1(30) = +$359.8B; DC the same way = +$193.5B. Add each to its base.","mental":"Work in changes, not levels — six products a market, not twenty-four. 6×28 = 168 and 3×51 = 153 are 89% of IRA's growth. Then anchor: 10% of $3,000B is $300B, so $360B is 12%.","check":"$359.8B on $2,996B is 12.0% over five years, about 2.3% a year; DC is 6.5%, about 1.3%. This is a choice between two slow markets, not a fast one.","matters":"The casebook compares levels — $3.36T against $3.19T — when the question asked about growth. They rank the same way only because both started at $3T."}},"BTH-19":{"decision":{"concept":"Not a money question at all: why junior salespeople leave within a year while the ones who last three years almost never go. The canvas is being used on a case with no arithmetic in it.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, in words only — 'heavy attrition' is qualitative until you ask, and the numbers you then get are tenures, not dollars.","matters":"Two groups doing the same job, one leaving and one staying, points at what differs between them — their pay — rather than at anything company-wide."},"rev":{"concept":"Not the client's revenue. Here it is what a single sale is worth, because the rep's commission is a slice of it — a high-margin device earns the seller more than a cheap one.","litAt":"step 3 · Structure","from":"Never given. The only prices in the case are an Apple analogy the interviewer offers — iPods at $100-300, iMacs at $2,000-3,000 — not the client's own products.","matters":"If juniors are only allowed to sell the cheap end of the range, this box explains the whole case without anything else being wrong."},"cost":{"concept":"The costs that matter here are one line of the P&L — what a salesperson is paid. The box is the suspect, not a number to be shaved.","litAt":"step 2 · The facts","from":"Never quantified. The case says a rep stays under a year and takes six months to get up to speed, and that reps over three years barely leave. No pay figure appears anywhere.","matters":"Turning the cost box from 'what we spend' into 'what they earn' is the move that makes this case solvable — it is the same money seen from the other side."},"vol":{"concept":"How much a rep sells, which the case names as the first driver of commission and then never measures. Territory, client type and product access all sit under it.","from":"Never given. The case asks how territories are assigned and whether the big accounts are reserved for seniors, but supplies no answer to either.","matters":"If seniors hold the high-volume accounts, juniors earn less for the same effort — the case's leading hypothesis rests entirely on a box that stays empty."},"price":{"concept":"The rate per unit here is the commission a rep earns on one sale — plus a small fixed salary underneath it, which is the part that does not move.","litAt":"step 3 · Structure","from":"Assumed by the case, not given: salespeople are 'typically paid a small fixed salary and a commission'. That is an industry generalisation standing in for the client's actual scheme.","matters":"If commission does not rise with the effort a sale takes, juniors selling hard, cheap products are underpaid by design — and design is fixable."},"opex":{"concept":"The running cost of a sales force, split base versus variable. The split is the whole case: the fixed part is small, so a junior's income is mostly commission.","litAt":"step 2 · The facts","from":"Never split with numbers. The framework asks for 'base and variable salary differences' between junior and senior reps; that comparison is listed as a next step, not performed.","matters":"A pay scheme that is mostly variable moves risk onto the newest people, who are also the worst placed to carry it. That is a structural answer, not a morale one."},"profit":{"concept":"Left dark on purpose, and worth noticing: nobody ever asks what the attrition costs. A case about a problem that never prices the problem.","from":"Never computed. The case gives the two figures that would start it — six months to reach full productivity, under twelve months of tenure — and uses neither.","mental":"Six months inside twelve is one half - read it as a fraction, do not divide. And if tenure is under a year, the learning share is over half, so the rounding runs against the firm.","check":"Ours: roughly half of a junior's time with the firm is spent learning. The case prints both numbers and never puts them together.","matters":"Without it there is no size to the prize, so the recommendation to rebuild junior pay is made with no idea what it is worth — the case's own framework asserts the answer before any evidence."}},"BTH-20":{"decision":{"concept":"Not 'are we losing money'. The client is profitable and says so; the question is why three years of growth produced no extra profit, and the answer is a fleet they bought.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1. The prompt never mentions freight — the candidate has to find the segment, and the interviewer only hands over Exhibit 1 when asked for it.","matters":"The answer is a retreat, not a cost cut: carry the freight in passenger bellies, where the space is already paid for, and consider selling the freighter business."},"rev":{"concept":"Freight revenue, and it rises — up 29% in three years. Growing sales are why the client believes the business is doing well, and why the problem stayed hidden for three years.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3, the freight bars: $212.5M, then $225M, then $275M. The client's own 'business is doing well' comes from this line and nothing else.","mental":"212.5 to 275 is up 62.5, close to a quarter of the base, call it 29%. Profit 200 to 120 is down 80, two fifths, so 40%. You need neither exactly, only that the signs oppose.","check":"Freight profit across the same three bars falls $200M to $120M. Revenue up 29% against profit down 40% is the whole diagnosis, visible before any per-flight arithmetic.","matters":"Rising revenue is what disguises the problem. The one box the client was watching is the one box that looks fine."},"cost":{"concept":"What it costs to fly one aeroplane, and it does not move: $400K for a freighter, $600K for a passenger jet, 'regardless of the freight carried'. Empty space costs what full space costs.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 5 in the sentence printed above Exhibit 2, not on the exhibit itself. What the nine extra freighters cost to buy is never given at any point in the case.","matters":"Because cost per flight is fixed, profit per flight is decided entirely by how full the hold is. That one fact converts a utilisation percentage into a loss."},"vol":{"concept":"Not units sold but how full the hold is — the share of a freighter's cargo space that someone paid for. It fell from 85% to 35% while the fleet went from 3 aircraft to 12.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 4 in the Additional Details box, alongside the belly hold's fall from 85% to 60%. Nothing in the case says freight demand fell; the capacity was multiplied under it.","mental":"Planeloads actually flown: 12 x 0.35 = 4.2 against 3 x 0.85 = 2.55. Round the base to 2.5 and 4.2 over 2.5 is 1.68, so about two thirds more capacity for 29% more revenue.","check":"Ours: 12 freighters at 35% is 4.2 planeloads against 3 at 85% = 2.55, so capacity flown rose 65% while freight revenue rose 29%. The case never sets the two side by side.","matters":"This is the box that explains the others. Nothing about price or cost changed; only the denominator did, and the per-flight loss follows straight from 35%."},"price":{"concept":"There is no price per ton here. Exhibit 2 gives revenue per flight at a given fill, topping out at $800K for a full freighter — so 'price' in this case means what a full aeroplane is worth.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 2's revenue ladder at step 5. The text only writes the $800K down at step 6, inside its own multiplication; it is never labelled as a full-load figure.","computes":"The ladder steps $200K per quartile, so $8K per point of fill. $800K × 85% = $680K then; $800K × 35% = $280K now.","mental":"Spread $800K across 100 points of fill and a point is $8K. Then 85% of 800 is 800 less 15%, so 800 - 120 = 680; and 35% is a third and a bit of 800, 280.","matters":"The ladder is straight, so revenue tracks fill and nothing else. That is what lets one utilisation figure decide profit with no pricing discussion at all."},"opex":{"concept":"The whole of this case's cost side: $400K to fly a freighter, incurred whether the hold is full or empty. There is no cost per ton of cargo anywhere in the case.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 5, above Exhibit 2. It is a running cost per flight, not per year — the case never totals it across the fleet or across a year, so no annual figure exists.","matters":"It is fixed, so it is the line the revenue ladder has to clear. At 85% full it was cleared by $280K; at 35% it is missed by $120K."},"capex":{"concept":"The one-off spend that caused the problem, and the case never prices it. The fleet went from 3 freighters to 12; what those nine aircraft cost is never asked, so this box holds a count.","litAt":"step 4 · Two holds","from":"Given at step 4 as a count only: 'quadrupled their number of freighters from 3 to 12 over the past 3 years'. No price, no lease terms, no depreciation, anywhere.","matters":"The absence is worth noticing. The case diagnoses an over-investment and then judges it only by the per-flight loss; a reader who wants the return on nine aircraft has nothing to compute it with."},"profit":{"concept":"Two different profits share this box. Exhibit 1 shows the freight segment's profit for a year; step 6 shows profit on a single freighter flight. The case moves between them without marking it.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit 1","from":"Computed at step 6. The casebook writes it '$400K − $280K = $120K loss' — cost minus revenue, the reverse of the usual order — and calls the $280K of revenue what the airline is 'making'.","computes":"$680K − $400K = +$280K a flight before. $280K − $400K = −$120K a flight now, a swing of $400K.","mental":"Keep the order revenue minus cost both times or the sign flips: +280, then -120. The swing is 280 + 120 = $400K, which is just the 50 points of fill lost at $8K each.","check":"Exhibit 1 puts freight profit at $200M on $212.5M of revenue — a 94% margin, impossible under this case's own $400K-a-flight economics. The two exhibits do not describe the same business.","matters":"$120K lost on every departure is what makes 'fly fewer freighters' the recommendation. The belly holds already have the room, and at 60% full they are giving it away."},"pay":{"concept":"There is no payback period in this case, because nothing was ever priced. What stands in its place is the load a freighter must carry to cover the cost of its own flight.","litAt":"step 6 · Per flight","from":"Ours. The case gives the $400K cost and the straight revenue ladder at step 5 and never puts them together; 50% is the point where the two meet.","computes":"$400K ÷ $8K per point of fill = 50% of the hold. At 85% the flight cleared it comfortably; at 35% it is 15 points short.","mental":"Break-even fill is cost over the value of one point: 400 over 8 is 50, so half the hold. Once you hold '$8K a point', every fill question is one small division away.","matters":"It reframes the case in one number: the fleet is not too expensive, it is too empty, and every freighter has been flying below its own break-even line."}},"BTH-21":{"decision":{"concept":"Two questions, not one: how many school buildings to reopen by next month, and how to make the teaching better. Only the first has any numbers behind it.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The deadline then moves three times — 'the next couple of months', 'in 2 months', 'by the end of next month' — and every demand figure is monthly.","matters":"The answer, 10 schools now and about 2 a month after, is purely a capacity sum. The quality half gets a brainstorm and no measure, so nothing could ever show it worked."},"rev":{"concept":"Nothing arrives here as money. The inflow this box holds is children: 24,000 already back, and 1,600 more every month with no stated end.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3. The slide says 24,000 have returned 'to the state'; the worked answer restates that as returned to New Orleans and counts every one as needing a district seat.","mental":"No sum, a denominator question: 24,000 counts everyone back in the state, but seats are for city district pupils. Shrink that top number and every school count below it shrinks.","check":"No enrolment or geography filter is applied anywhere: children back in Louisiana but outside the city, no longer school age, or going private all count as demand.","matters":"It is the only demand figure in the case, so every school in the answer is sized off it. Overstated, the district builds seats it cannot fill and cannot fund."},"cost":{"concept":"Raised in the framework and never sized. Reopening buildings, recruiting teachers nationally and improving facilities all cost money, and no figure appears in five pages.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Nowhere. Step 1 says the district faced significant financial problems and was on the verge of bankruptcy, and no budget or per-school figure ever follows.","matters":"The plan commits to 10 schools at once and about 2 every month indefinitely, unfunded. 'Lack of funding' sits third in the risk list; it is the starting condition."},"vol":{"concept":"Seats needed at a moment in the future: every child already back, plus every child who will arrive while the buildings are still being unlocked.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Built at step 5. Counting two months of arrivals — 'current month + next month' — is the case's own choice, and the 24,000 carries no measurement date.","computes":"24,000 + (2 × 1,600) = 27,200 students. Against 18 schools × 1,000 seats: 6,000 + 3,200 = 9,200 short → 10 new schools.","mental":"Work in thousands and the case does itself: 24 plus 3.2 is 27.2 needed, 18 already open, so 9.2 short. Dividing by 1,000 only moves the point, which is why 1,000 was chosen.","check":"9,200 ÷ 1,000 = 9.2, rounded up to 10. The ongoing rate rounds the same way — 1,600 ÷ 1,000 = 1.6 becomes '~2 a month', some 400 empty seats every month for good.","matters":"Counting two months of arrivals rather than one is what turns a 6-school answer into a 10-school one. The horizon is the case's most sensitive input and is never pinned down."},"price":{"concept":"No money changes hands, so what sits in this per-unit slot is capacity: 1,000 children per building, the divisor behind every school count in the case.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 4 from the old system — 64,000 students across 64 schools. It is an average of what used to be, not a standard anybody chose.","computes":"64,000 ÷ 64 = 1,000 students per school.","mental":"64,000 over 64 is the digits cancelling to 1, so 1,000. Then 18 schools holding 24,000: 18 x 1,300 is 23,400, so about 1,333 each, a third above the design figure.","check":"The 18 schools already open hold 24,000 children, about 1,333 each. The recommendation notes they are over capacity and still sizes new buildings at 1,000.","matters":"The framework asks outright whether 1,000 is the goal or it should be higher; the answer uses 1,000 without replying, and the quality half asks for smaller classes."}},"BTH-23":{"decision":{"concept":"Not a yes or a no. The dinosaur's asking price is the unknown, so the case solves for the largest cheque that still leaves the project worth doing.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt as two questions. The guidance supplies the rule: Lola Lo should buy at any price that leaves net present value positive.","matters":"What comes out is a ceiling, not an offer — $13.125M is where the deal stops being worth doing, and the case never says what she should actually bid."},"rev":{"concept":"Six ways the dinosaur brings money in — gate, hotel, licensing, park food, merchandise, research — handed over as six finished annual figures.","litAt":"step 3 · The stream","from":"Given as a list at step 3. No visitor count and no ticket price ever appear, so the $2M of ticket sales is asserted whole and cannot be checked.","computes":"$2M + $1M + $0.5M + $0.5M + $0.25M + $0.25M = $4.5M a year.","mental":"Group the equal fractions before the big numbers: 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 and 0.25 + 0.25 = 0.5. So 2 + 1 + 1 + 0.5 = $4.5M.","matters":"Held flat for 29 years by instruction — 'assume revenues and costs are static' — which is what lets one year's profit stand in for the whole life."},"cost":{"concept":"Two unlike things in one box: $1.5M every year to keep the dinosaur, and $15M once to build its exhibit. The yearly one is subtracted, the one-off is saved for the end.","litAt":"step 3 · The stream","from":"Both given at step 3. The casebook files the three annual lines under 'Variable Costs' though none of them moves with visitor numbers — they are flat.","computes":"$1M staff + $0.25M food & merchandise + $0.25M operations = $1.5M a year; plus $15M once for the exhibit.","mental":"Two quarters make a half, so $1M + $0.5M = $1.5M. And spot the cancelling pair first: $0.25M of merchandise cost against $0.25M of merchandise revenue, both gone.","check":"The $0.25M merchandise cost exactly cancels the $0.25M merchandise revenue, so that pair adds nothing at all to the answer.","matters":"Keeping the $15M out of the yearly figure is what makes the last step possible: value the annual stream first, then take the build off it."},"vol":{"concept":"Here the volume box holds years, not customers. Nothing in this case is ever counted in visitors, so the quantity that scales the answer is how long the animal lives.","litAt":"step 2 · Life","from":"The casebook prints 'the next 29 years' and never shows the subtraction; the two ages behind it are given at step 2, once the candidate has suggested ways to find them.","computes":"Expected lifespan 50 years − current age 21 = 29 years.","mental":"50 - 21: step to the round number first. Take 20 off to reach 30, then one more, 29. Going via 30 means the borrow never happens.","matters":"This case stops the stream at the animal's death rather than running it forever, and that stopping is the thing it is teaching."},"opex":{"concept":"The recurring half of the cost box: feeding and staffing the dinosaur, running the exhibit, and the cost side of the food and merchandise counters.","litAt":"step 3 · The stream","from":"Given flat at step 3, never split by visitor numbers and never grown for inflation across the 29 years.","computes":"$1M + $0.25M + $0.25M = $1.5M a year.","mental":"Two quarters make a half: $1M + $0.5M = $1.5M. Quarters are worth recognising on sight rather than adding up as 0.25s.","matters":"It is the only thing taken off the $4.5M, so it alone fixes the $3M that every later step is built from."},"capex":{"concept":"Building the exhibit and the housing: $15M paid once, before a ticket is sold. Separate from the dinosaur's own price, which is the thing being solved for.","litAt":"step 4 · Solve","from":"Given at step 3 among the facts, as a one-time fixed cost. Nothing supports the figure — no quote, no comparison — and the risks page admits the exhibit's cost is unknowable.","matters":"It comes straight off the value of the 29-year stream, so every dollar of exhibit cost is a dollar less that can be offered for the animal."},"profit":{"concept":"One year of running the exhibit — and by instruction all 29 years look exactly like this one. This flat $3M is the stream that gets discounted.","litAt":"step 3 · The stream","from":"Computed at step 4 from the two given lists; both the workings and the recommendation print $3M.","computes":"$4.5M revenue − $1.5M costs = $3M a year, for 29 years.","mental":"$4.5M - $1.5M = $3M. The halves cancel, leaving 4 - 1. When both numbers end in the same fraction, subtract it away first and the rest is whole numbers.","matters":"Its flatness is what lets the case value the project with one division instead of 29 rows of cash flow."},"pay":{"concept":"What 29 years of profit are worth in today's money, less what must be spent to collect them — which is the most Lola Lo can pay for the animal itself.","litAt":"step 4 · Solve","from":"The casebook gives the 10% discount rate at step 4 and the $13.125M on the recommendation page, but never prints the arithmetic in between.","computes":"Set NPV to zero: value of the stream − $15M exhibit − price = 0, so price = value of the stream − $15M.","mental":"$3M / 0.10 multiplies by ten, it does not divide: $30M. Rule of 72 says 10% doubles in about 7 years, so 29 years is four doublings - the tail is $30M / 16 = $1.875M.","check":"Ours: $3M forever at 10% is $30M, less the tail beyond year 29. Taking 1.1^29 ≈ 16 gives $30M − $1.875M = $28.125M, − $15M = $13.125M. Discounted exactly: $13.11M.","matters":"The dinosaur dies, so the stream stops. Cutting that tail off the perpetuity is worth $1.875M, and making that subtraction is the whole method here."}},"BTH-24":{"decision":{"concept":"The prompt is vague on purpose and the first mark is for sharpening it: how do we earn most from Zewal in the months of monopoly left, and what carries the firm for five years after?","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, which closes three branches in four sentences: costs are already lean, mergers are impossible, the next blockbuster is more than five years away.","matters":"Naming the window — under a year — bounds every idea that follows. Anything that only starts paying after the patent expires is a different proposal and must be argued as one."},"rev":{"concept":"A revenue line with a date on it. Half the company's money comes from one molecule whose legal monopoly ends within a year; the other half is spread across ten drugs.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt as shares only. No dollar figure for revenue appears anywhere in this case — not once, on any of its five pages.","matters":"Because it is a share and not a sum, no idea in this case can be sized. The grade is the structure that generates the ideas, not a number at the end of them."},"cost":{"concept":"Closed by a single sentence. The company is efficient, well run and has been cutting for years — so the cost branch of the profit tree is shut before you finish drawing it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given in the additional information, released in discussion. The interviewer guidance says explicitly to redirect a candidate who pushes on cost.","matters":"This is the sentence that makes it a revenue case. Four minutes on a cost branch are four minutes spent on a branch the interviewer is already waiting to close."},"vol":{"concept":"Volume here is prescriptions, and doctors write them — patients do not buy the drug directly. So reach into the profession is the nearest thing to a volume figure on offer.","litAt":"step 3 · Customers","from":"Given at step 4 as 'our client's reach is about 10%', against a rival's 35%. The case never states how many doctors exist, nor how many prescriptions one writes.","check":"Ours: 150 reps × 100 doctors = 15,000 covered. If that is about 10%, the profession is roughly 150,000 — a number the case implies everywhere and states nowhere.","mental":"Dividing by 10% is multiplying by ten: 15,000 → 150,000. Never divide by 0.1; shift the decimal instead, and decide which way it should move before you move it.","matters":"20% of doctors write 80% of prescriptions — about 30,000 on the implied count, and we can cover only 15,000. Reassigning reps costs nothing and is never once proposed."},"price":{"concept":"Never given, never discussed, and not a lever. Once the patent ends anyone may copy the molecule, and a copy costing pennies to make collapses the price.","litAt":"step 6 · Rivals","from":"Inferred from step 6's one fact — about six months after expiry before generics arrive. The case supplies no price and no elasticity at any point.","matters":"Price is the branch the case silently deletes. Seeing that leaves volume, inside a closing window, as the only thing left in the profit equation to work on."},"opex":{"concept":"The salesforce is the only cost the case quantifies, and only as headcount: 150 people covering 100 doctors each, against an industry norm of one rep per 50.","litAt":"step 4 · Salesforce","from":"Given at step 4 if asked. No salary, no cost per rep and no revenue per rep is ever supplied — so the case's own 'stop when marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue' rule cannot be run.","computes":"150 reps × 100 doctors = 15,000 doctors covered — the only multiplication this case makes possible.","check":"Ours: 15,000 ÷ 50 = 300 reps to reach the industry ratio. That is double today's force, for exactly the same 15,000 doctors and exactly the same 10% reach.","mental":"15,000 ÷ 50 is 15,000 ÷ 100 doubled: 150, then 300. Halving the divisor doubles the answer — faster and far harder to fumble than dividing by 50 directly.","matters":"The two benchmarks answer different questions: 1-per-50 is how often each doctor is visited, 35% is how many are visited at all. Going to 300 reps changes reach by nothing."},"profit":{"concept":"Contribution margin appears once, as a rank rather than a number: Zewal earns the least per sale in the whole portfolio, and reps are paid on contribution margin.","litAt":"step 5 · Pay plan","from":"Given at step 5 if asked. No margin figure for any drug is stated — only that Zewal's is lowest, and that pull has so far substituted for push.","matters":"The pay plan is pointing the salesforce away from the one drug with a deadline. That is the cheapest and fastest lever in the case, and the recommendation reaches it last."}},"BTH-25":{"decision":{"concept":"Move one machine's production to India — but the machine is a test, not the point. Behind it sits $300M of cost the CEO wants to send abroad on the same reasoning.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, which asks for pros and cons before any data arrives. That order is deliberate: you are being made to structure with no numbers to hide behind.","matters":"The XL292's own saving is 0.2% of profit and decides nothing. The real question is whether a rate measured once, on one machine, holds across a cost base fifty-five times larger."},"rev":{"concept":"The company's whole sales line, and it appears only as a yardstick. No revenue is ever attached to the XL292 itself.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt with the 12% net margin. The two exist to be multiplied together once, and then to judge every later figure against.","computes":"$3B × 12% = $360M of net profit a year.","mental":"12% is a tenth plus a fiftieth: $300M + $60M = $360M. Splitting an awkward percentage into 10% plus a bit is faster and far harder to get wrong.","matters":"Without it, $45M is a number with no scale. Against $360M it is 12.5% of profit — big enough to act on, which is the only reason the recommendation exists at all."},"cost":{"concept":"The annual bill for building 10,000 XL292s, landed: everything up to the machine being available to use, whether made in the town or shipped from India.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 4 from Exhibit 1. It excludes the idle American capacity the framework itself warned about, and the $2.5M cost of moving the line.","computes":"$540 × 10,000 = $5.4M staying local. $460 × 10,000 = $4.6M from India. Saving $0.8M a year.","mental":"Multiplying by 10,000 is four zeros and nothing else: $540 becomes $5,400,000. Do the per-unit stack while the numbers are small, and scale at the very end.","check":"The saving is a small difference between two large numbers. Four of the six differing lines are worth more than $80 a unit on their own, so any one can swing it.","matters":"$0.8M is 0.2% of company profit, so this machine alone cannot justify anything. What travels to the $300M base is the rate, not the money."},"vol":{"concept":"Machines a year — and it is identical in both columns. The case compares two ways of making the same quantity, not two different quantities.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 1","from":"Read off the top row of Exhibit 1. Whether a brand-new Indian line could reach 10,000, or reach it in year one, is never raised anywhere.","check":"31,250 units of breakeven ÷ 10,000 a year = 3.1 years. The casebook prints the 31,250 and never performs this division.","mental":"31,250 over 10,000: knock four zeros off both and it is 3.125. Dividing by ten thousand is moving a decimal point four places, nothing more.","matters":"It is the missing scale on the breakeven. '31,250 units' means nothing to a CEO; 'three years of production, then $0.8M a year' is a decision."},"price":{"concept":"Empty, and worth noticing. No selling price for the XL292 appears anywhere, because the case treats the machine purely as a cost to be reduced.","from":"Nowhere. The nearest thing to a customer-facing number is the $85 'Made in USA' credit per unit, and the case never says what it is or who pays it.","matters":"With no price the case cannot ask whether moving production changes what buyers will pay, or whether a machine built in India sells for what one built in the town does."},"opex":{"concept":"The all-in landed cost of one machine: labour, materials, holding, freight, duty, less a domestic credit. Six lines, and only three of them are factory costs.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 1","from":"Built line by line from Exhibit 1 at step 4. Every figure checks. The $150 materials gap, the largest single line of the saving, is never explained.","computes":"US: $30×6 = $180, then +400 +30 +15 +0 −85 = $540. India: $7×8 = $56, then +250 +20 +44 +90 −0 = $460.","mental":"Never compare the hourly rates. $30 against $7 looks like a quarter, but India needs 8 hours to America's 6, so it is $56 against $180 — under a third, not a quarter.","check":"Factory lines save $284 a unit (labour $124, materials $150, holding $10). Distance and policy hand back $204 (freight $29, duty $90, lost credit $85). Net $80.","matters":"Forget the $85 credit and America costs $455, India looks dearer, and the recommendation reverses. One row of the exhibit, and the answer flips."},"capex":{"concept":"What it costs to pick the production line up and set it down eight thousand miles away. It appears once, in an optional question, and then vanishes.","litAt":"step 7 · Breakeven","from":"Given inside Question 3, which the interviewer is free to skip. It is not in Exhibit 1, not in the $0.8M, not in the $45M, and not in the recommendation.","matters":"The recommendation says the machine's savings 'do not seem high enough to merit overseas investment' — a judgement about an investment it never puts beside the saving."},"profit":{"concept":"Company-wide profit, used only as a denominator. The case never computes a profit for the XL292, because it has no price to compute one from.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Computed at step 1 from the prompt's $3B and 12%. Every figure the case asks you to judge — $0.8M, $45M, $12M — is judged against it.","check":"$0.8M is 0.2% of it; $45M is 12.5%; $12M is 3.3%. The casebook calls that last one '3% of current profit margin', which is a different and much smaller claim.","mental":"Share of profit, fast: 10% of $360M is $36M, so $45M is a bit over a tenth — 12.5%. Anchor on 10% and adjust; that is enough to know whether a number matters.","matters":"It sorts three figures into one that justifies nothing, one that justifies a programme, and one that justifies a pilot. The recommendation is a direct reading of that row."},"pay":{"concept":"How long the move takes to repay itself, counted in machines rather than months — because the only benefit the case has is a per-unit one.","litAt":"step 7 · Breakeven","from":"Computed at step 7 from the $2.5M and the $80 saving. No discount rate is given or applied, so a dollar saved in year three counts as a dollar today.","computes":"$2,500,000 ÷ $80 = 31,250 units, or 3.1 years at 10,000 a year. The same answer arrives as $2.5M ÷ $0.8M a year.","mental":"$2.5M ÷ $80: divide both by 10 until the divisor is a single digit — 250,000 ÷ 8 = 31,250. One easy division beats one awkward one.","check":"At Question 2's doubled Indian wage the saving is $24 a unit, so $2.5M ÷ $24 = 104,167 units — ten and a half years. The two optional questions are never combined.","matters":"Three years or ten years is the difference between a pilot worth running and one that is not, and both numbers sit on the same page without ever meeting."}},"BTH-26":{"decision":{"concept":"What a fully approved vaccine is worth in the US. The client asks for revenue potential; the case ends up computing what the health system would spend, which is not the same thing.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt; 'potential' is clarified on request as revenue potential plus a cost-benefit analysis. Development cost is ruled out of scope on the data sheet.","matters":"The answer is one segment, not the whole eligible population — $300M, not $3B. The recommendation then adds a second $300M its own decision rule rejects."},"rev":{"concept":"Money to the client, and the lesson is which slice of it is real: only the babies for whom vaccinating beats treating, which turns out to be the smallest group.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 5. The $6,000 per child is the administration cost reused as the client's price, so this 'revenue' is the payer's spend with nothing added on top.","computes":"50,000 babies under 1,000g × $6,000 = $300M a year.","mental":"5 x 6 = 30, then count the zeros back: four and three is seven, so 30 with seven zeros, $300M. Digits first and zeros last is exactly where a factor of ten goes missing.","check":"The recommendation quotes $300M–$600M. The second $300M is the 1,000–1,500g segment, where the case's own rule picks antibiotics at $125M over vaccine at $300M.","matters":"$3B is what the eligible population would cost; $300M is what a payer would rationally buy. The distance between those two numbers is the entire case."},"cost":{"concept":"Cost here belongs to whoever pays for treatment, not to the client: $6,000 to protect one baby, set against antibiotics for the few in that group who would fall ill.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 5 from the dose cost and the segment sizes. Nothing the client spends — manufacturing, distribution, development — appears anywhere in the case.","computes":"All 500,000 low-birth-weight babies × $6,000 = $3B; by segment, $300M + $300M + $2.4B.","mental":"Ten times the babies is ten times the spend, so $3B follows from the $300M with no new work. The 10/10/80 split then cuts the $3B the same way: $300M, $300M, $2.4B.","check":"Vaccine cost falls on 100% of a segment while antibiotic cost is weighted by infection rate. That is right — you cannot know in advance who falls ill — and the case never says so.","matters":"Laid beside $905M of antibiotics across all three segments, $3B is what kills the big number. Only in the smallest group does prevention cost less than treatment."},"vol":{"concept":"Babies eligible each year, and then the far smaller number actually recommended: 50,000 in the under-1,000-gram group out of 500,000 born under weight.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 3 from two given figures — 5 million US births a year, 10% of them low birth weight. Exhibit 1 then splits that 500,000 into 10% / 10% / 80%.","computes":"5,000,000 × 10% = 500,000 babies; × 10% / 10% / 80% = 50K, 50K and 400K.","mental":"A tenth of 5 million is 500,000, one move of the point. Then 10/10/80 of that is 50K, 50K and 400K, and they must add back to 500K before you use any of them.","check":"50K + 50K + 400K = 500K — the segments close. The largest is eight times the first and carries the lowest infection rate of the three, 2%.","matters":"Sizing all 500,000 gives $3B and is wrong. The recommendation rests on 50,000, a tenth of the population, and the arithmetic of narrowing it is the case."},"price":{"concept":"$6,000 for a complete course of six shots. Not a price set from what the vaccine is worth — it is the cost of administering it, handed straight back as the price.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 3: $1,000 a dose and a six-dose course are both given, and question 1 answers the pricing question with 'at or above the cost of administration'.","computes":"6 doses × $1,000 = $6,000 per child.","mental":"Six thousand-dollar doses is $6,000, and the ease is the warning: the sum hides that the price was set equal to the cost, so nothing at all is earned on top of it.","check":"One flat price across all three segments although the value differs seventyfold — $14,000 of expected antibiotics avoided in the first group, $200 in the third.","matters":"Six is the multiplier candidates drop, and dropping it divides every figure by six. Pricing at cost is the quieter error: it leaves the client nothing per child."},"opex":{"concept":"$1,000 to get one shot into one baby — the only unit cost in the case, and it is a cost of delivering care, not a cost of making the vaccine.","litAt":"step 3 · $3B","from":"Given on the data sheet at step 3, on request. What it covers is never broken out, and the client's own cost to produce a dose is never given at all.","matters":"It does double duty as the payer's cost and as the client's price, which is why cost to vaccinate and revenue potential both land on exactly $3B."},"capex":{"concept":"There is none, and the case says so out loud: 'R&D considerations — no, not for this case'. The vaccine arrives fully developed and fully approved, with nothing sunk.","from":"Ruled out on the data sheet at step 3, alongside efficacy (assume 100%) and approval risk (fully approved). No launch or manufacturing spend appears either.","matters":"Stripping development out is what lets $300M read as a prize rather than a return on something. Nothing here can say whether this vaccine was worth building."},"profit":{"concept":"The client's margin, which the case never supplies. Price was set equal to the cost of administration, so on the case's own figures the client earns nothing per child.","litAt":"step 7 · Answer","from":"Never given. The prompt promises revenue potential and a cost-benefit analysis for the client; every figure computed after the framework belongs to the payer instead.","matters":"$300M of revenue at an unknown margin is not a decision. The case cannot say what launching is worth to the client, and never notices the gap."},"pay":{"concept":"What the spend buys back — the antibiotics avoided, weighted by how likely each group is to fall ill. This is the true ceiling on price, and it varies wildly by segment.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit","from":"Ours, not the case's. Every input sits in Exhibit 1 — infection rates 40%, 10%, 2% and treatment costs $35K, $25K, $10K — and the case never multiplies them.","computes":"Ours: 40% × $35K = $14,000; 10% × $25K = $2,500; 2% × $10K = $200 of expected cost avoided per child.","mental":"40% of $35K is 4 x 35 = 140, so $14,000. 10% of $25K is one move of the point, $2,500. 2% of $10K is a fifth of 10%, so $200. Each group is worth roughly a tenth of the last.","check":"Against a flat $6,000 price: worth paying in the first group, more than twice too dear in the second, thirty times too dear in the third.","matters":"These three numbers answer the pricing question the case asks on page three and then abandons. Segment pricing is never raised, though the data for it is on the exhibit."}},"BTH-29":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether a private equity fund should buy Gargoyle. The fund's test is stated up front and is narrower than 'is this a good company': grow EBITDA every year, with little capital spending.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1. The price is never given anywhere in the case, so 'should we buy' can only be answered about the business, never about the deal.","matters":"The stated test decides it. A brand whose EBITDA has already stopped growing fails a requirement written as 'grow EBITDA each year' before any valuation is attempted."},"rev":{"concept":"Bottles of shampoo times what a bottle fetches, in two channels at two different prices. The $50M of new revenue that appeared in 2010 is the entire subject of this case.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3 as bars. Exhibit 3 splits it by channel at step 5 and the two reconcile to the dollar — though the case never asks anyone to check that they do.","computes":"2009: 20M bottles × $7.50 = $150M. 2010: 20M × $7.50 + 10M × $5.00 = $200M.","mental":"20 x 7.5 is 20 x 7 plus 20 halves: 140 + 10 = 150. Halve and double works too, 10 x 15 = 150. Then the new channel adds 10 x 5 = 50, giving $200M.","matters":"Revenue grew a third and the company earned not one dollar more. This box is why growth on its own is never an answer to 'is it worth buying'."},"cost":{"concept":"The cost of one drugstore bottle, not a company total. Gargoyle's overall cost base never appears — only what it costs to make and move a single bottle into the new channel.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given in Exhibit 4 at step 6, per bottle. Watch the unit: $4.50 is per bottle, and the $5.00 printed alongside it is the drugstore price, not a cost.","computes":"$5.00 price − $4.50 variable cost = $0.50 left on each bottle, a tenth of the sale.","mental":"Fifty cents left on a five-dollar bottle is a tenth, so 10%. Read any 'x out of ten x' as 10% on sight; it is the fastest sanity check this case offers you.","matters":"Fifty cents is thin enough that the channel's own fixed cost swallows all of it. Everything the case concludes comes out of this one subtraction."},"vol":{"concept":"Bottles, not dollars — the distinction this case punishes hardest. 20 million in 2009, 30 million in 2010, and all ten million extra are drugstore bottles.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit 2","from":"Exhibit 3 at step 5 gives the split as percentages of two different totals; the totals are 20M and 30M bottles. Converting to bottles before reading is the trap the exhibit sets.","computes":"2010: about a third of 30M = 10M drugstore bottles; two thirds = 20M salon bottles, identical to 2009's 20M.","mental":"A third of 30M is 10M and two thirds is 20M. Turn the percentages back into bottles before comparing years, or you are comparing shares of two different totals.","matters":"Salon volume flat at 20M is what kills the growing-company story. Every extra bottle came from the one channel that earns nothing."},"price":{"concept":"Two prices for the same shampoo: salons pay $7.50, drugstores $5.00 — a third less. The case presents the gap as a fact rather than as a decision somebody made.","litAt":"step 5 · Exhibit 3","from":"Given in Exhibit 3's right-hand column at step 5. Exhibit 4 then supplies a cost for the drugstore bottle; no cost is ever given for the $7.50 salon bottle.","matters":"The cheaper channel is the only one with a cost attached, and it is the one that fails. What the salon bottle actually earns is never shown, so the profitable half is the unexamined half."},"opex":{"concept":"The cost of existing in drugstores at all — the channel's fixed cost, not the bottles'. It exists only because the channel was entered, which is what makes testing the channel against it fair.","litAt":"step 6 · Exhibit 4","from":"Given in Exhibit 4 at step 6, labelled incremental. A yearly figure standing alone; the company's other fixed costs are never given, so this cannot be added to anything.","mental":"10M x $0.50 is simply half of 10M, so $5M. When both sides of a comparison land on the same figure, suspect design rather than coincidence, and say so out loud.","check":"The channel's contribution was 10M bottles × $0.50 = $5.0M — the same $5.0M. Two different quantities that come out equal, which is this case's whole design.","matters":"Set against fifty cents a bottle it fixes break-even at ten million bottles, and ten million is exactly what the channel sold."},"capex":{"concept":"The price of Gargoyle, and it is never stated. An acquisition decision with no purchase price in it, so no return on the money can be worked out at any point.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"The case never says. The prompt asks whether to invest and stipulates 'minimal capital expenditure', but no figure is ever attached to either the price or the spending.","matters":"Worth noticing rather than assuming you missed a page. The case is decided purely on the quality of the business — and a low enough price would have made the same business a yes."},"profit":{"concept":"EBITDA: cash profit before the accounting deductions. It is flat to the dollar across 2009 and 2010, and it is flat because $50M of new revenue arrived carrying no profit with it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Exhibit 1 prints revenue as bars and margin as a percentage line at step 3. The dollars have to be multiplied out — the figure $30M is never printed anywhere.","computes":"20% of $150M = $30M in 2009. 15% of $200M = $30M in 2010. The same number twice.","mental":"20% of 150 is a fifth, 30. 15% of 200 is 10% plus half of it again: 20 + 10 = 30. Same answer twice, so the margin fall is one $30M spread over more revenue.","check":"The margin fall is arithmetic, not decay: the same $30M spread over a third more revenue. Nothing in the salon business got worse.","matters":"'Grow EBITDA each year' is the fund's own stated test, and this box has already failed it once before the fund has done anything at all."},"pay":{"concept":"Not payback in years but in bottles: how much the drugstore channel must sell before it adds anything. This case's test of an investment is a quantity, not a date.","litAt":"step 6 · Exhibit 4","from":"Computed at step 6 from Exhibit 4's two figures. The casebook prints both steps of it and both check out.","computes":"$5.0M fixed ÷ $0.50 a bottle = 10M bottles to break even; 10M × $5.00 = $50M of revenue.","mental":"Dividing by 0.50 is doubling, never halving: $5M becomes 10M bottles. Then 10M x $5 = $50M. Flip that and break-even looks four times easier than it really is.","check":"Exhibit 3 already said the channel sold 10M bottles for $50M. It landed exactly on its break-even — not below it.","matters":"Exactly at break-even means $50M of sales and zero profit. The casebook says 'not profitable'; precisely, it added nothing, and it diluted the margin on everything else."}},"BTH-30":{"decision":{"concept":"How much to bid for broadcast rights. The case builds all nine boxes to produce one number — the most the network could pay and still be no worse off for having bid.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1. The $177M arrives at step 8 as the end of the chain; nothing in the prompt says the answer should be a ceiling rather than an offer.","computes":"$928M revenue − $428M cost − $146M forgone = $354M in 2018 money, halved for six years at 12% = $177M.","mental":"Chain it in one unit, millions: 928 - 428 = 500 exactly, then less 146 is 354. The halving for six years comes last, never first. Discount the net, not the parts.","matters":"Bid the ceiling and the network captures none of the value it just computed. The recommendation says 'Enter a bid of $177M' — the one bid at which winning is worth nothing."},"rev":{"concept":"Advertising, and only advertising. The client is stated to make money from ads and not subscriptions, so sixteen days of Olympics are worth exactly the ad slots inside them.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 5 from the schedule and the slot prices, in four rows. Nothing is taken off for slots that go unsold — every slot is assumed to sell at the rate card.","computes":"$240M weekday non-prime + $320M weekday prime + $320M weekend + $48M ceremonies = $928M.","mental":"Pair the equal numbers first: two 320s make 640, plus 240 is 880, plus 48 is 928. Adding the odd one last beats reading straight down the column.","check":"Ours: 146 hours × 10 minutes is over 24 hours of advertising, all sold at full price, with no unsold inventory and no make-goods. The case never tests that assumption.","matters":"Every figure downstream is a subtraction from this one, so an error here moves the bid proportionally. It is also the only box built entirely from the client's own rate card."},"cost":{"concept":"Two unlike things sit here. $428M is money that leaves the network; $146M is revenue it never earns, because Olympic hours displace the programming that would have run instead.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"The $428M is simply asserted at step 6, with no breakdown, after the candidate has brainstormed cost categories. The $146M is given at step 7 as $1M per displaced hour.","computes":"$428M paid out + $146M given up = $574M taken off $928M of revenue.","mental":"428 + 146 is easier as add 150 and give 4 back: 578 - 4 = 574. Round to the friendly number and adjust, and you carry once instead of twice.","matters":"The $146M has no box of its own on this canvas — it is neither opex nor capex, it is revenue forgone. Miss it and you stop at $500M, and at $250M rather than $177M after discounting."},"vol":{"concept":"Hours of broadcast — the thing being sold, in a case with no units and no customers. Every dollar of revenue is an hour multiplied by what an hour of ads fetches.","litAt":"step 3 · Calendar","from":"Built at step 3 from the schedule table. The case refuses to show the table's arithmetic and expects the candidate to organise the hours himself — that is the exercise.","computes":"Weekdays 10 × (6 non-prime + 4 prime) = 100; weekends 4 × 10 = 40; ceremonies 2 × 3 = 6. Total 146 hours, of which 86 are prime.","mental":"10 weekdays x 10 hours is 100, weekends 40, ceremonies 6, so 146. Prime is 40 + 40 + 6 = 86, and 86 of 146 is just under 60%, since 60% of 146 would be 87.6.","check":"Prime is 59% of the broadcast, which is why the average hour earns $6.4M ($928M ÷ 146) rather than the $4M non-prime rate.","matters":"The same 146 returns at step 7 as the hours of ordinary programming displaced. One count does both jobs, so a wrong calendar moves revenue and opportunity cost together."},"price":{"concept":"What an hour of Olympics is worth: a slot price times how many slots fit in an hour. The ceiling on slots is set by viewers' patience, not by the network.","litAt":"step 4 · Ad load","from":"Built at step 4 from two given figures — consumers will take no more than 10 minutes of ads an hour, and a slot is 30 seconds. Twenty slots, and no more.","computes":"10 minutes ÷ 30 seconds = 20 slots. Non-prime: 20 × $200K = $4M an hour. Prime: 20 × $400K = $8M an hour.","mental":"Ten minutes holds twenty half-minutes. Then 20 x $200K is 2 x 2 with the zeros put back, $4M an hour, and prime simply doubles it to $8M.","matters":"Prime is worth exactly double, so the shape of the schedule — 86 of the 146 hours in prime — does more for the bid than the length of the Games does."},"opex":{"concept":"The whole cost of putting the Games on air — studios, talent, infrastructure, hotels, insurance — handed over as one number, after you have listed what belongs inside it.","litAt":"step 6 · Costs","from":"Given flat at step 6: 'There is no breakdown here… state that the total accounting cost is $428M.' Nothing fixed, nothing variable, nothing tested against anything.","mental":"928 - 428 needs no working: the 28s cancel, leaving 900 - 400 = 500. A subtraction that clean usually means the figure was chosen rather than measured.","check":"Ours: $928M − $428M = $500M exactly. The cost looks chosen to make the accounting profit a round number rather than measured from anything.","matters":"It is the only cash cost in the case, so it alone turns $928M of revenue into the $500M that the case calls accounting profit — the figure many candidates stop at."},"capex":{"concept":"The bid itself belongs here: the one-off price of the asset. In this case it is the unknown — the box the whole canvas is being used to fill, rather than an input to it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Never given, by design. The case is 'how much should they bid', so the box stays empty until step 8 puts a ceiling of $177M into it.","matters":"Reading it as empty is right until the last step. It is also why no payback or return appears anywhere: with no price paid there is nothing to divide the profit into."},"profit":{"concept":"Economic profit, not accounting profit: what is left after paying for what the Olympics displace as well as for what they cost. The case walks you through the accounting figure first on purpose.","litAt":"step 6 · Costs","from":"Computed at step 7. The $500M accounting profit at step 6 is laid as a trap — the case says many candidates will think it is the answer and stop there.","computes":"$928M − $428M = $500M accounting profit; $500M − $146M forgone = $354M economic profit, in 2018 money.","mental":"500 - 146 is 500 less 150 with 4 given back, so 354. Then test the $146M: at the case's own $4M and $8M an hour, a forgone hour should cost far more than $1M.","check":"The $146M values all 146 displaced hours at $1M each, prime and daytime alike — though the case's own rates say a prime hour earns $8M against $4M off-peak.","matters":"$354M, not $500M, is what the rights are worth in the year they run. Skipping the displacement leaves you at $500M, 41% too high before a dollar has been discounted."},"pay":{"concept":"The same profit expressed in 2012 money — this case's stand-in for payback. The Games are six years out, and at 12% money doubles in about six years, so halve it.","litAt":"step 8 · Answer","from":"Computed at step 8. The 12% discount rate is given only here, in the last step, and the case never says where it comes from or whose rate it is.","computes":"72 ÷ 12 = 6 years to double; the cash is 6 years out; so $354M ÷ 2 = $177M in 2012 dollars.","mental":"Rule of 72: 72 over 12 is 6, so money doubles in six years and six years back is one halving. $354M becomes $177M. Exactly it is $179M, so the rule costs $2M.","check":"Exactly, $354M ÷ 1.12⁶ = $179M. The rule of 72 is about $2M conservative here — close enough to use, and worth knowing that it is an approximation.","matters":"This is the ceiling, and the case turns it straight into the recommended bid. A ceiling and an offer are not the same number, and paying the ceiling earns the network nothing."}},"BTH-31":{"decision":{"concept":"Why Quahog's children score below the national average — except the case does not answer that. It is steered onto one asserted cause, a book shortage, and sizes a fix for it.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The books diagnosis arrives at step 4 as an interviewer's aside — 'early findings suggest' — with no data, no exhibit and no analysis behind it.","matters":"By the last page that aside has become 'shortage of books is the leading indicator of subpar performance', while the risks beside it concede other causes may matter more."},"rev":{"concept":"Nothing is earned here. What flows in is books recovered — 4,000 of the 8,000 missing, put back into children's hands by two schemes that need no cash.","litAt":"step 6 · Impact","from":"Computed at step 6. Each scheme is assumed to act on one subject only, and the two are asserted to be 'independent', which is what licenses adding them together.","computes":"70% × 4,800 maths = 3,360; 20% × 3,200 science = 640; 3,360 + 640 = 4,000 books.","mental":"Take both as percentages of the whole hole: 70% of 60% is 42%, since 7 x 6 = 42 and both are tenths. 20% of 40% is 8%. 42 + 8 = 50%, half, from two unequal halves.","check":"Shortcut: 70%×60% + 20%×40% = 42% + 8% = 50%. Averaging the two rates to 45% is the trap — the slices they act on are different sizes.","matters":"4,000 of 8,000 is the case's entire quantified result, and it is a count of books — never converted into a single point of test score."},"cost":{"concept":"Eight ways of getting books are brainstormed and not one is priced, in a case whose very first idea is 'raise money to buy books'.","litAt":"step 3 · Framework","from":"Nowhere. No budget, funding source or per-book figure appears in six pages, and the two schemes that get quantified are chosen by the interviewer, not by comparison.","matters":"Without a cost the case cannot say the chosen pair is the best pair, only that it is the pair it was handed. The recommendation rests on a choice it never justifies."},"vol":{"concept":"The size of the hole: 8,000 grade-6 maths and science books that should exist and do not. Every percentage in the case is applied to this one number.","litAt":"step 3 · Framework","from":"Given at step 5, inside the follow-up question. No school count, pupil count or books-per-pupil figure appears anywhere, so the 8,000 has no denominator.","computes":"60% × 8,000 = 4,800 maths books; 'the rest', 40% × 8,000 = 3,200 science books.","mental":"Anchor on 10%: a tenth of 8,000 is 800, so 60% is six of them, 4,800, and the rest is 3,200. Check the two halves add back to 8,000 before applying anything to either.","check":"4,800 + 3,200 = 8,000 — the split closes, which is worth confirming before you apply anything to either half.","matters":"8,000 short of 10,000 needed and 8,000 short of 200,000 needed are different worlds, and nothing distinguishes them. A grade-6 figure then reports as a district result."},"price":{"concept":"Not a price. The per-unit rate here is how much of each subject's shortage a scheme can close: 70% of maths through Amazon, 20% of science through sharing.","litAt":"step 5 · Shortage","from":"Given at step 5. The maths restriction is explained — science books are not sold on Amazon — but no reason is ever given why sharing between schools should be science-only.","matters":"Two rates on two differently sized slices produce the whole answer. If sharing also reached maths the total would exceed 4,000 and the headline 50% would be too low."},"profit":{"concept":"What is left over is a shortage, not money: 4,000 books still missing after both schemes have done everything they can do.","litAt":"step 6 · Impact","from":"The mirror of the recovered figure at step 6. The case states it directly in its own risk list as 'remaining shortage of 4000 books'.","computes":"8,000 − 4,000 = 4,000 books still short.","mental":"The subtraction is trivial; the fraction is the point. Half the hole recovered means half still open, so the fix and the failure are the same size. Say it that way, not as 4,000.","matters":"The case's first risk is that this half 'will not be easily addressed'. Half the problem survives the recommendation, with no plan beyond a next step."},"pay":{"concept":"What the effort buys back: a rise in test scores. It is the one thing the client actually asked for, and the only box the case never fills with anything.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit","from":"Never. Exhibit 1 shows two unlabelled bars — no axis, no scores, no year, no sample — and after 'Show Exhibit 1' it is not referred to again.","matters":"Books are never converted into scores in either direction, so the case cannot say how much its recommendation helps the thing it was hired to fix. That is a next step."}},"BTH-33":{"decision":{"concept":"Two questions asked in order: why did $5M of profit become a $5M loss, and should the two distributors change? The first is about costs, the second about growth, and they share no numbers.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. Price and product mix are closed off at step 2 by instruction — one undifferentiated product, no room to move price — so only the cost side is live.","matters":"The answer turns out to be a batch size. A setting chosen to make an inventory statistic look better is what pushed the division's profit below zero."},"rev":{"concept":"Flat, and shut as a lever before the case begins: one undifferentiated product, one price, a market where three firms hold about a quarter each.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given at step 2 as 'about $100M per year', then printed exactly on Exhibit 1 at step 3 as $100M, $101M, $101M.","check":"The case says price never changed and Exhibit 2 says volume is 50M yards every year — yet revenue moves. On its own figures the implied price goes $2.00 → $2.02.","mental":"Strip matching units before dividing: $100M over 50M yards is just 100 ÷ 50 = $2. Then 101 ÷ 50 is 2 plus 1/50, and 1/50 is two cents.","matters":"With revenue fixed, nothing can be blamed on demand. Every dollar of the swing from +$5M to −$5M has to be found on the cost side of the same page."},"cost":{"concept":"Total spend, and the shape of the problem: it rises $11M while revenue rises $1M. One of its three parts does almost all of the moving.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Ours — Exhibit 1 never totals its cost lines. This is COGS plus manufacturing cost plus SG&A, read off the exhibit at step 3.","computes":"2004: $40M + $40M + $15M = $95M. 2006: $42M + $49M + $15M = $106M.","mental":"Never total both columns and subtract. Difference them line by line: COGS +2, SG&A 0, manufacturing +9. The $11M is 9 plus 2, and the 9 is the case.","matters":"Manufacturing supplies 80% of the increase, which is what sends the case to Exhibit 2 and the machine rather than to raw materials or the sales force."},"vol":{"concept":"Yards of canvas, identical in all three years. That constancy is the case's control: if output never changed, then whatever got worse got worse per yard.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 2's top row at step 4 — 50M yards in 2004, 2005 and 2006 alike.","computes":"Runs per year is 50M ÷ run length: 50,000 runs at 1,000 yards, 100,000 runs at 500. The same yards, twice as many setups.","check":"The casebook prints 'efficiency is down 33%' twice. Its own figures give 16.67 → 12.5 yards a minute, which is a fall of 25%.","mental":"Cancel zeros: 50M ÷ 1,000 = 50,000. And time and speed are reciprocals — 60 minutes becoming 80 is +33%, and 1 ÷ 1.333 = 0.75, so the two percentages can never match.","matters":"Same output, twice the stops. That sentence is the finding, and it is available only because volume is held constant while everything else moves."},"price":{"concept":"Never printed anywhere, and declared immovable: an undifferentiated product against two rivals of equal size means nobody can charge a cent more.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Ours — no price appears in the case at all. It is revenue divided by yards, and the claim that price never changed sits oddly beside the $1M revenue rise.","matters":"Closing this box is what makes the case a pure cost problem. It also means the efficiency gain must show up as cost saved — and the case never prices a minute of machine time."},"opex":{"concept":"The one line that moves. Not materials — COGS is nearly flat — but the cost of running the machines: more employees, more overtime, the identical quantity of canvas.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit 1","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 3. Exhibit 2 then supplies the physical cause: employees 100 → 120, and overtime hours each 100 → 300.","computes":"$40M → $44M → $49M: up $9M, or 22.5%, against output that never moved at all.","mental":"Anchor on the easy fraction and step down: a quarter of 40 is 10, so 9 on 40 is a little under a quarter — 22.5%.","matters":"The $9M is what the answer has to explain, and no wage rate or cost per machine-minute appears anywhere, so the explanation stays physical and is never sized in money."},"profit":{"concept":"Net income for this division: a $10M swing on a revenue line that moved $1M. Everything else in the case exists to explain that gap.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Read off Exhibit 1's bottom row at step 3. The prompt says only that profitability 'declined'; the size of the decline arrives with the exhibit.","computes":"$101M − ($42M + $49M + $15M) = −$5M in 2006, against $100M − $95M = +$5M in 2004.","mental":"Add the two big ones first: 42 + 49 is 91 (take 50, give back 1), plus 15 is 106. Then 101 − 106 = −5.","matters":"A division of a $12B parent crossing into loss is what buys the attention, and the entire crossing is manufacturing cost — the box next door, not this one."}},"BTH-34":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to fix the smart-card business or leave it. The CEO has already named the exit in the prompt, which is unusual — the case is not asking how to grow, it is asking whether to stay.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1, with the spin-off or sale named among the follow-up details. No financial target, no hurdle rate and no deadline is attached to it.","matters":"The answer is sell to Competitor A or B. Read everything below that way: the case is not hunting for the fix, it is testing whether a fix would be worth having."},"rev":{"concept":"Never sized. The market is never measured and the client's own revenue is never given, so this box stays a word — 'low growth' — for the whole case.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given qualitatively in the prompt at step 1: 'low revenue growth and recent customer complaints'. No figure is offered at any point, by any exhibit or question.","matters":"The absence is deliberate. With no revenue there can be no cost-benefit on the $10M, and the case says as much — a strong candidate is expected to decide without one."},"vol":{"concept":"Customers, counted — not units. Ten big-city transport authorities and seventy mid-sized ones, and each runs one supplier, so a customer is won whole or not at all.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given in Exhibit 1 at step 4 as segment sizes. The segmentation itself is the candidate's to propose at step 3; the interviewer steers him to revenue-based tiers if needed.","computes":"Tier-1: 10 customers × 100% penetration — all ten have already bought. Tier-2: 70 × 10% = 7 have bought, 63 have not.","mental":"10% of 70 is 7, leaving 63 untouched. The rebase is the real move: 2% and 8% of all seventy are shares of the same seven buyers, so they read as 20% and 80%.","check":"Tier-1's shares add to 100% (shares of the ten buyers); Tier-2's add to 10% (shares of all seventy). Rebased, the seven Tier-2 buyers split 20% A and 80% B — never printed.","matters":"Ten customers, all already taken, is the fact that decides the case: Tier-1 share can only move by taking a whole city off a rival who is already more reliable."},"price":{"concept":"There is no price in this case. The interviewer states that all three players are at parity on everything except reliability, which retires price as a lever in a single sentence.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 5, in the note under the purchase-driver question: 'all players in the market have parity on everything except reliability'. Never quantified.","matters":"Parity on price is what leaves reliability as the only gap — and the only gap turns out to cost $10M to close and to buy a tie."},"capex":{"concept":"One number, arriving late: $10M to lift reader reliability from 90% to 99%. It is the only investment the case ever prices, and it buys a specification, not a customer.","litAt":"step 7 · The $10M","from":"Given at step 7 inside Question 5, after the reliability table. Nothing says over what period it is spent, or whether it recurs, or what it does to card reliability.","matters":"99% is exactly Competitor B's reader reliability. The $10M buys the client level with the leader on its one weak driver — level with a rival holding 45% of Tier-1."},"profit":{"concept":"The box the prompt makes you expect and the case never fills. The CEO is 'unhappy with the performance' of this business and no profit, margin or revenue figure is given for it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Never given. The only numbers in the case are customer counts, penetration and share percentages, reliability percentages, and the $10M at step 7.","matters":"A reader who notices this box stays dark has seen the case's real move: with no arithmetic available it decides on structure alone — three players, ten saturated accounts, one winner per city."},"pay":{"concept":"What the $10M earns back, answered in words. Not a period and not a rate — the return here is a position: equal to the best rival instead of behind it.","litAt":"step 7 · The $10M","from":"Argued at step 7, never computed. The case explicitly expects the answer without a cost-benefit, because the revenue to set against the $10M was never supplied.","matters":"Spending $10M to draw level, in a market where every Tier-1 account already belongs to someone and winning one means displacing an incumbent, is the argument for selling rather than fixing."}},"BTH-35":{"decision":{"concept":"Not 'why did the margin fall' but 'where can three years of profitable growth come from'. The margin decline is the constraint every idea must survive, not the question.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. Notice what is never given: no cost for any growth option, so the whole case is decided on margin percentages alone.","matters":"The client asked for growth that is profitable. The small-school arithmetic shows growth that is not — which is why the answer becomes a new way of selling, not a bigger push."},"rev":{"concept":"Premiums from about 500,000 students at large universities — a fifth of the students in the market but nearly a quarter of its money.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Both figures given in the prompt, rounded with a '~'. The case never breaks $500M down by product or by school, so nothing below it can be checked against it.","check":"$500M of $2.2B is 22.7% of the money; 500K of 2.5M is 20% of the customers. So our students pay slightly more than the market average.","mental":"500 over 2,200: anchor on 10% = 220, so 500 is a bit over twice that — just under a quarter. Then 500K of 2.5M is a clean fifth. Anchoring on 10% beats long division.","matters":"Leader, but of a fragmented market — four fifths of the students are someone else's. That is why 'go down-market' looks obvious before the margins are checked."},"cost":{"concept":"Every cost here arrives already expressed as a share of revenue, so the sums are addition and nothing else. Four rows: medical, selling, setup, admin.","litAt":"step 4 · Criteria","from":"Data Sheet 2 at step 5 prints each row both as a percentage and in dollars, so nothing has to be derived — only added and compared to 100%.","computes":"Option 1: 80% + 7% + 5% + 2.5% = 94.5% of revenue, leaving 5.5%. The worst case, option 4, reaches 102%.","mental":"Add the two frozen shares first: 80 + 7 = 87. Then the two that move: 5 + 2.5 = 7.5. 87 + 7.5 = 94.5, so 5.5 is left. Grouping by what can move shows the real room.","matters":"87 of every 100 dollars is committed before the client does anything at all. The entire case lives inside the 13 points that remain."},"vol":{"concept":"Students, not schools — the student is the one who actually pays. Penetration is 20%, and in a market sold school by school that means schools we are not inside at all.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. Data Sheet 2's 'customers' column is a different quantity — students per school, 250 to 1,000 — and the digits 500 appear in both places.","check":"Data Sheet 2, option 1: $1,000,000 ÷ $2,000 = 500 students at one school. That is not the 500K national figure, despite the identical digits.","mental":"Dividing by $2,000 is halving then striking three zeros: 1,000,000 → 500,000 → 500. Say the unit aloud at each step or the two 500s quietly merge.","matters":"Volume is the lever the case hands you and then removes: with medical cost fixed at 80% of revenue, more students cannot widen the margin, only spread the $50K setup."},"price":{"concept":"Two plans, $2,000 and $1,000 a student a year, and the client may move neither — the recommendation page says a price rise is not possible under health care reform.","litAt":"step 2 · Growth","from":"Printed on Data Sheet 2 at step 5 as 'Plus ($2,000)' and 'Standard ($1,000)'. Handed over, never argued for.","check":"$500M ÷ 500K customers = $1,000 average — exactly the cheaper plan, so the existing book must be almost entirely Standard.","mental":"$500M over 500K: strike five matched zeros off each and it becomes 500 ÷ 0.5 = 1,000. Cancelling matched zeros first turns a frightening division into a small one.","matters":"The dearer plan halves the variable share — admin costs 2.5% of a $2,000 premium but 5% of a $1,000 one — so price is what separates a 5.5% option from a 3.0% one."},"opex":{"concept":"Not costs the client manages. 80 cents of every premium dollar must by law be spent on medical care, and 7 cents more goes on selling however the business is run.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"The 80% is given in the prompt as a reform requirement; the 7% selling line appears only on Data Sheet 2. Neither is ever justified, and neither varies across the four options.","computes":"80% + 7% = 87% of revenue, identical in all four scenarios.","mental":"Both are shares of the same revenue, so they simply add — no common denominator to hunt for. 87% means 13 cents in the dollar is all there is to fight over.","matters":"This is the ceiling. Best case the client keeps 13%, and setup and admin come out of that — which is why no small-school scenario reaches the 9% it already earns."},"capex":{"concept":"The cost of the 'first sale' — winning the campus health centre before a single student can be sold to. Flat per school: the same $50,000 at a college of 250 as at one of 1,000.","litAt":"step 4 · Criteria","from":"Data Sheet 2 prints it as a percentage (5% or 10%) and as $50K. The flat dollar figure is the fact; the percentage moves only because school revenue does.","computes":"$50,000 ÷ $1,000,000 = 5%; $50,000 ÷ $500,000 = 10%. Same cost, twice the bite at a school half the size.","mental":"How many 50s in 1,000? Twenty — so a twentieth, 5%. Halve the revenue and the share doubles to 10%. Every fixed cost behaves this way; learn the shape once.","matters":"$50,000 to open a school is bearable at 1,000 students and fatal at 250. It is the one cost the client may legally attack, and attacking it means not selling school by school."},"profit":{"concept":"What is left after all four cost rows. The test the case sets is not 'is it positive' but 'does it beat the 9% the client already earns'.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"9% and 25% given in the prompt; the four small-school margins are computed on Data Sheet 2 at step 5. The case never explains the 16-point fall.","computes":"100% − 94.5% = 5.5% for option 1, then 3.0%, 0.5% and −2.0% as the cost rows worsen.","check":"Ours: the sheet charges the whole $50K setup to one year. At $10K a year over five, the four margins become 9.5%, 7.0%, 8.5% and 6.0% — option 1 now clears the 9% hurdle.","mental":"Setup falls from 5% of revenue to 1%, so add 4 points; at the $500K schools it falls from 10% to 2%, so add 8. Move the margin by the change, never recompute the column.","matters":"The casebook rejects the small-school market because none of the four beats 9%. That verdict rests entirely on a one-year amortisation the sheet never states."},"pay":{"concept":"Not years here but students: how big a school has to be before the flat $50,000 setup is covered by what each student leaves behind.","litAt":"step 5 · Margins","from":"Ours — the case never computes it. Admin runs at $50 a student throughout the sheet ($25K at 500 students, $12.5K at 250), which is what makes a per-student figure possible.","computes":"Plus: $2,000 less 80% medical, 7% selling and $50 admin = $210 a student; $50,000 ÷ $210 = 238 students. Standard leaves $80, so 625.","mental":"10.5% of $2,000 is $210: take a tenth, $200, then half of a twentieth, $10. Then 50,000 ÷ 210 is a shade under 50,000 ÷ 200 = 250, so 238.","matters":"It names the client's real constraint: below 238 students no small school can pay on the Plus plan however well it is run. The margin table hides that structural fact."}},"BTH-37":{"decision":{"concept":"Where to put one factory — Brazil, France or the US. The unusual part: the site does not only change what building costs, it changes how much you are allowed to sell.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt as two questions — what the parameters for the choice should be, and then where. The first is scored as heavily as the second.","matters":"The arithmetic ends 3% apart, so it cannot decide. The answer comes from the capability branch — politics, labour, quality — which looked like decoration until that moment."},"rev":{"concept":"Government spending, not customer demand. One buyer per country, its budget published, and it only buys from a factory standing on its own soil.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Exhibit 1 at step 3, as percentages of three defence budgets. That a country buys nothing at all from a foreign plant is printed as 0% and never defended.","computes":"20% × $100B = $20B from the US, identical in all three rows. 50% × $15B = $7.5B from Brazil; 50% × $10B = $5B from France.","mental":"Percentages of different bases do not compare. 50% of Brazil's $15B is $7.5B — far less than 20% of America's $100B. Turn both into dollars before you rank them.","check":"The US column is the same $20B whichever site you pick, so one whole column of the exhibit contributes nothing to the decision.","matters":"The entire revenue difference between the two foreign options is $7.5B against $5B — $2.5B. That narrowness is why the case ends up decided on risk."},"cost":{"concept":"Four stacked lines per site: building it, five years of fixed cost, a variable cost per aircraft, and a border charge that exists only if you build abroad.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Built at step 5 from figures handed over on request. The $15M certification charge is the one line a candidate must think to ask for; it sits under 'additional cost'.","computes":"US $0.5B + $0.5B + $3B + $0 = $4B. Brazil $2B + $0.5B + $5.5B + $3B = $11B. France $3B + $0.5B + $6.25B + $3B = $12.75B.","mental":"Variable cost belongs to where a thing is made, not where it goes, so all 275 Brazilian aircraft cost $20M each: 2 × 275 = 550, put the zero back, $5,500M = $5.5B.","check":"The $3B certification is 200 × $15M, not 275 × $15M. Only aircraft entering the US pay it; the 75 Brazilian ones never cross a border.","matters":"Costs are what beat France: it earns $2.5B less than Brazil and spends $1.75B more. An option losing on every live term can be deleted without any arithmetic at all."},"vol":{"concept":"Helicopters — and the split matters more than the total. 200 are always American-bound; the extra 75 or 50 stay in whichever country built them.","litAt":"step 5 · Units & costs","from":"Computed at step 5 by dividing each demand figure by the $100M price. Write it as 200+75 rather than 275, because the split is needed twice afterwards.","computes":"$20B ÷ $100M = 200 helicopters. $7.5B ÷ $100M = 75. $5B ÷ $100M = 50.","mental":"Billions over millions: cancel three zeros off each side and it becomes $20,000M ÷ $100M = 200. Divide the digits first, restore the zeros afterwards.","matters":"Certification is charged on the 200 entering the US, not on all 275. Charging the other 75 adds $1.1B to Brazil's costs and reverses the recommendation on its own."},"price":{"concept":"One price to every buyer, never negotiated. Its real job is not revenue — it turns a government budget into a count of aircraft, and costs are charged per aircraft.","litAt":"step 5 · Units & costs","from":"Given at step 5, on the cost page rather than the revenue one. The casebook steers candidates towards asking for it and mis-states why they need it.","computes":"Price cancels: ($20B ÷ $100M) × $100M = $20B. Revenue here is the government's budget, whatever the price happens to be.","mental":"Spot the round trip before doing it. Divide by a number, then multiply the answer by that same number, and you are back where you started — so price cannot move revenue.","matters":"Its one live use is the unit count, which carries $15–25M of variable cost and $15M of certification apiece. The next step — would Brazil pay less? — only strengthens the US answer."},"opex":{"concept":"The yearly bill for keeping a plant open, and it is $100M wherever the plant stands. A row of identical numbers cannot distinguish between the options.","litAt":"step 4 · Cost brainstorm","from":"Given at step 5 with the other cost lines. The case never explains why running a plant should cost the same in São Paulo as in Kansas.","computes":"$100M × 5 years = $500M, at all three sites.","mental":"An identical row is free: compute it once, copy it across three columns, and stop looking at it. It still gets subtracted; it can never change the ranking.","matters":"Real money and decision-irrelevant at the same time. Spotting that early is what leaves time for the variable and border costs, which do differ and do decide."},"capex":{"concept":"The one cheque written before anything is built — and the only three numbers in the case that never get multiplied by anything else.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given at step 5, but the $500M is already in the prompt's background: one US plant can take an extra assembly line for $500M, and the other two cannot expand at all.","matters":"The US option is a bolt-on to a working plant; Brazil and France are factories from nothing. Same row of the table, three quite different kinds of risk."},"profit":{"concept":"Five years of profit per site. The gap between the top two is half a billion on sixteen — small enough that the arithmetic cannot settle anything.","litAt":"step 6 · Profit","from":"Computed at step 6 by subtracting each cost column from its revenue column. Every figure reconciles to the casebook exactly, which is not always true in this corpus.","computes":"$20B − $4B = $16B. $27.5B − $11B = $16.5B. $25B − $12.75B = $12.25B.","mental":"Rebuild the gap from its parts, not from two big totals: Brazil's extra 75 net $6B; the penalty on the American 200 plus the bigger plant is $5.5B; $0.5B left. 0.5 on 16 is a 32nd, 3%.","check":"$0.5B ÷ $16B = 3.1%, the casebook's '3% difference'. France loses on revenue, on setup and on variable cost, so it could have been struck out unpriced.","matters":"A 3% gap sits well inside the error of every assumption behind it. That is what licenses the recommendation to be made on risk instead, and the case says so openly."},"pay":{"concept":"Not a payback period — the case never asks when the money returns. Ours instead: how hard each dollar of plant has to work to produce the profit above it.","litAt":"step 7 · Risks & rec","from":"OURS, not the casebook's. Built at step 6 from figures the case prints. The case compares profit totals only, and calls the US option 'slightly less profitable'.","computes":"$16B ÷ $0.5B = 32× for the US. $16.5B ÷ $2B = 8.25× for Brazil. $12.25B ÷ $3B = 4.08× for France.","mental":"16 ÷ 0.5 is 16 × 2 = 32 — dividing by a half doubles. For 16.5 ÷ 2, just halve: 8.25. Turn division into doubling or halving whenever the divisor is a round fraction.","matters":"It reverses the framing. Brazil is 3% more profitable and four times less efficient with capital, and a recommendation resting on risk alone leaves that argument unused."}},"BTH-38":{"decision":{"concept":"Keep the trucks and drivers in-house, or hand the shipping to an outside firm at $8.50 a pound. Nothing about the boards changes — only who moves them.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The guidance adds the constraint that makes the case work: assume no difference in revenue, so the answer has to come out of costs alone.","matters":"The verdict is keep it in-house. The book scales the daily gap to about $49K a year ($133 × 365 = $48,545), a figure that inherits the doubt about the $133.","mental":"Read them as a fraction, not a gap: 15 and 25 share a 5, so it is 3 to 5. Outsourcing breaks two-thirds again as much, which is far bigger than 'ten points' sounds.","check":"Damage is the hinge: 15% in-house against 25% outsourced. The interviewer says both out loud; neither appears on the exhibit."},"rev":{"concept":"Deliberately held still. Same boards, same customers, same prices either way — the case rules revenue out so the whole decision has to settle inside costs.","from":"Not a figure at all. The guidance says 'there should be no assumed difference in revenue'. No selling price for a whiteboard appears anywhere in the case.","matters":"Because revenue is frozen, the 10 extra pounds that survive in-house are counted but never valued — which is why the saving has to be faked in dollars later."},"cost":{"concept":"Running the trucks: maintenance, fuel, wages. No truck is ever bought or sold here, so cost means one day of operating, never the fleet capital tied up in the yard.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Read off Exhibit 1 at step 2 as three bars. The outsourced side is not an exhibit — it is $8.50 a pound applied to the day's 100 pounds.","computes":"In-house: $260 maintenance + $350 fuel + $240 salaries = $850/day. Outsourced: $8.50/lb × 100 lb = $850/day.","mental":"Add the hundreds first: 260 + 350 = 610, then + 240 = 850. And $8.50 x 100 only moves the point two places, $850. The match is exact, so check it rather than trust it.","check":"The two come to the same $850. Every dollar of the answer therefore comes from the damage rates, not from the cost bars.","matters":"Identical totals are the point: stop at cost per day and you find no difference at all, and the case has no answer to give."},"vol":{"concept":"Not boards sold — pounds that arrive unbroken. 100 pounds set off each day; how many are still worth selling at the end is the only thing separating the two options.","litAt":"step 3 · Per good pound","from":"Built at step 3 from the 50 lb shipped twice, cut by damage rates the interviewer states out loud (15% in-house, 25% outsourced) rather than printing on the chart.","computes":"100 lb − 15% = 85 good lb in-house; 100 lb − 25% = 75 good lb outsourced.","mental":"Percentages of 100 are the free case - the number is the answer. 15 off leaves 85, 25 off leaves 75. Subtract from 100 rather than multiplying by 0.85.","check":"The 100 lb is 50 lb of boards moving two legs, so the same boards are counted twice; '85 saleable white boards' is really 85 pound-legs.","matters":"This is the only box where the two options differ, so the entire recommendation rests on a gap of 10 pounds a day."},"price":{"concept":"Stays dark, and it is worth knowing why: the case does print a rate, $8.50 a pound, but that is what the vendor charges us. It is a cost rate, not a price we charge.","from":"No selling price for a whiteboard is ever given, so nothing can be built here.","matters":"Keeping it dark is what stops you valuing the 10 rescued pounds at a margin the case never supplies."},"opex":{"concept":"The whole of the cost box. Every dollar in this case is a running cost, paid again the next day; nothing at all is paid once.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit","from":"The three bars of Exhibit 1, which the interviewer notes are total costs, not per-pound costs — the commonest misreading of this chart.","computes":"$260 + $350 + $240 = $850 a day, in-house.","mental":"Round to make it easy: 250 + 350 + 250 = 850. Then adjust - you added 10 to maintenance and took 10 off salaries, so the two corrections cancel. $850 exactly.","matters":"Because it is the same $850 either way, this box decides nothing on its own; it only becomes an answer once divided by good pounds."},"profit":{"concept":"Despite the label, no profit is computed in this case. What sits in the box is cost per pound that survives the trip — the real yardstick here, and lower wins.","litAt":"step 3 · Per good pound","from":"Computed at step 3 by dividing each option's day of cost by its own good pounds. Both figures are printed on the calculations page.","computes":"$850 ÷ 85 = $10.00/lb in-house; $850 ÷ 75 = $11.33/lb outsourced; a gap of $1.33.","mental":"850 / 85 is 10 on sight, since 85 x 10 = 850. For 75: 75 x 11 = 825, leaving 25, and 25 is a third of 75, so $11.33. The gap is $1.33.","check":"The book then writes $1.33 × 100 lb = $133/day saved. But 100 lb is pounds shipped, not the good pounds $1.33 is measured in — and both options spend the same $850.","matters":"$10.00 against $11.33 is the whole answer: same money out, fewer boards standing, so outsourcing costs more per board that can actually be sold."}},"BTH-40":{"decision":{"concept":"Not a business decision — a defence. Is the plaintiffs' $1.2B damages figure a fair estimate of harm, and where exactly does it overstate?","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. Yarmouth's financials, strategy and operations are explicitly ruled out of scope, which is unusual and shapes everything that follows.","matters":"A damages number is a price move multiplied by a share count. You attack the two terms separately, never the total — and the two reductions multiply rather than add."},"rev":{"concept":"The plaintiffs' claim, built at its maximum: the whole of one day's fall applied to every share the company has ever issued and not bought back.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Given at step 5. Shares outstanding is a count of ownership at a moment; it says nothing about who bought when, or who was made worse off by anything.","computes":"$8.00 × 155MM = $1,240MM. The casebook prints $1.2B, rounding $40 million away without remarking on it.","mental":"8 × 155: double it three times — 310, 620, 1,240. Repeated doubling is safer than multiplying by 8, and it lands on $1.24B, not $1.2B.","check":"$8.00 ÷ 0.22 = $36.36, so the share stood near $36 before the fall — which is where Exhibit 2's line sits. The stated fall and the stated −22% agree.","matters":"It is the largest arithmetically available figure, not an estimate of harm. Saying that out loud is the whole answer to the interviewer's 'is this reasonable?'"},"cost":{"concept":"The defence's own damages figure — the same multiplication as the plaintiffs', with both terms replaced by ones that survive scrutiny.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Computed at step 6 from the two inputs beside it. The casebook's 'Calculations' box is printed blank in the source; only the $100MM appears, at step 7.","computes":"$4.00 × 25MM = $100MM, against $1,240MM claimed — a reduction of $1,140MM.","mental":"The two cuts multiply, they do not add: half of one term and a sixth of the other leaves a twelfth. 50% × 16.1% = 8.1%, and 8.1% of $1.24B is $100M.","check":"Cut the per-share figure alone and you get $620MM; cut the share count alone and you get $200MM. Neither argument is dramatic on its own; together they are.","matters":"Twelve-fold, from two ordinary arguments. That compounding is the transferable lesson: when two optimistic numbers are multiplied, attack both terms, never the product."},"vol":{"concept":"Not units sold — shareholders. The count shrinks because three groups on the register were never harmed, whatever happened to the price that day.","litAt":"step 5 · Their number","from":"155MM is given at step 5; the 25MM is simply asserted at step 6 with no working at all. The case's own risk list concedes plaintiffs will contest it.","computes":"25MM ÷ 155MM = 16.1%, so 83.9% of the claim disappears on one unexplained figure.","mental":"25 over 155: round 155 to 150, and 25 is a sixth of 150 — a sixth is 16.7%, so the real answer sits just under it, 16.1%. Round the base, then adjust down.","check":"The three exclusions are each sound — bought and sold before the disclosure, held by executives, or held right through the class period — and not one is quantified.","matters":"This is the largest lever in the case, bigger than the causation argument and bigger than the regression, and it is the one with no arithmetic behind it at all."},"price":{"concept":"Harm per share, which is not the price fall. It is the part of the fall the disclosure caused, once the market and the industry have been taken out.","litAt":"step 3 · Causation","from":"The $8.00 is read off Exhibit 2 and stated at step 5. The $4.00 modelled return is handed over at step 6 with no model shown and no source named.","computes":"$8.00 − $4.00 = $4.00 of inflation per share. The 'but-for' price is what the share would have been worth had nothing been disclosed.","mental":"Half of eight is four — and that is the point. The modelled return has been set at exactly half the fall, and a suspiciously round split is worth querying before using it.","check":"Step 3 concluded the indices were flat and did not explain the fall. If that holds, the modelled return is near zero and damages are nearer $8 × 25MM = $200MM.","matters":"The case's two headline findings contradict each other. On its own causation evidence the $4.00 cannot stand, and the defensible figure is about twice the $100MM reported."},"profit":{"concept":"What the two defences are worth between them: the gap between the claim and the number the defence would actually put in front of a court.","litAt":"step 6 · Our number","from":"Computed at step 6. The casebook states it only as '$100MM versus $1.2B' and never names either the difference or the ratio.","computes":"$1,240MM − $100MM = $1,140MM removed. $1,240MM ÷ $100MM = 12.4×.","mental":"For the ratio, ignore the units: 1,240 over 100 is 12.4 — move the decimal two places. The subtraction is the same shape: 1,240 − 100 = 1,140.","matters":"It is still $100MM, and the case says so: a charge that size is significant for this company. Winning by a factor of twelve is not the same as winning."}},"BTH-42":{"decision":{"concept":"Not 'should we buy FSC' but 'can FSC's margin be dragged from 10% to 40%'. What the company costs to buy is never mentioned, so this is a feasibility question wearing a deal's clothes.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"The 10%-to-40% target sits in the interviewer's guidance and is withheld unless asked. The prompt itself says only 'is there room to increase profitability'.","matters":"Every figure in this case is per APCD — one passenger, one day. Hold that unit and the case is simple; lose it and each number looks like a whole company's accounts."},"rev":{"concept":"One passenger, one day, all-inclusive: fare, cabin, food, drink and excursions in a single figure. Casino money is excluded by instruction, so there is nothing else to add.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3 as the industry's unit revenue, then held at exactly $1,000 for the rest of the case. Nobody tests whether it survives a fuller ship.","matters":"Revenue never moves, so every point of margin has to come out of the cost line. That is the case's deliberate design, and it is why the cost routing matters so much."},"cost":{"concept":"Seven lines, each posing the same question: should this look like the parent's number or a luxury competitor's? The case is won on the routing, not on the arithmetic.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Exhibit 1 itemises all seven FSC lines at step 3, and they sum to exactly the $900 given verbally. The $650 and $584 are computed at steps 4 and 6.","computes":"$100+$100+$50+$250+$50+$100+$250 = $900. After alignment: $10+$5+$45+$220+$50+$100+$220 = $650.","mental":"Group equal figures rather than reading down the column: three hundreds make 300, two 250s make 500, two 50s make 100. 300 + 500 + 100 = $900.","matters":"$900 down to $650 is $250 of saving and still only a 35% margin. The routing exercise, which is most of the case's work, does not reach the target on its own."},"vol":{"concept":"The bottom of the fraction. Every figure here is a total divided by passenger-days, so passengers per ship is the lever nobody touches for four steps.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit 3 at step 6: FSC carries 800 today and the survey says customers would accept 1,000. The case never prints the step between them.","computes":"800 → 1,000 is 25% more passengers, so ship-level cost per passenger becomes 800 ÷ 1,000 = 80% of what it was — a 20% cut.","mental":"Divide the old count by the new, not the new by the old: 800/1,000 = 0.8 straight off. Read it as '+25%' and take 1 ÷ 1.2 and you get 0.833, which is wrong.","matters":"This one move is worth $66 per APCD against the $250 won by negotiating every line, and it supplies the last five points that the alignment could not."},"price":{"concept":"The same $1,000, seen as a decision rather than a fact. A luxury line carrying 25% more people is a different product, and nobody asks whether the fare survives that.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3 and never revisited. The risks page raises the experience question in words and still leaves the fare untouched in the arithmetic.","check":"Ours: at a $950 fare the target fails — ($950 − $584) ÷ $950 = 38.5%. A 5% discount undoes the entire density gain.","mental":"No division needed. A tenth of 950 is 95, so 40% would be 380. The $366 left over is short of 380, so the margin must be under 40%.","matters":"The whole recommendation rests on a fuller ship still selling at the same price. It is the least examined assumption in the case and the cheapest one to break."},"opex":{"concept":"The five lines that belong to the ship rather than to the passenger — crew, fuel, docking, rent, head office. These are what dividing by more passengers actually dilutes.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Ours: the casebook never groups them. They are the aligned $10 + $5 + $45 + $220 + $50, and the 20% cut at step 6 applies to these five and to nothing else.","computes":"$10 + $5 + $45 + $220 + $50 = $330, and $330 × 0.8 = $264. Food ($100) and excursions ($220) follow the passenger and do not move.","mental":"Take 20% off by taking a tenth twice: a tenth of 330 is 33, twice is 66, and 330 − 66 = 264. Subtracting tenths beats multiplying by 0.8.","matters":"Knowing which lines are per-ship and which are per-passenger is the whole of step 6. Apply the 20% to all seven and you get $520 and a fictitious 48% margin."},"capex":{"concept":"Dark, and the case's own framework asked for it: 'will any capital investments be needed to reduce operating costs?' Adding 200 berths to every ship is exactly that.","from":"Never given. The 20% cut is treated as free — no refit cost, no shipyard time, no sailings lost while the work is done.","matters":"The five points that clear the 40% target come entirely from berths that do not yet exist. A margin bought with unpriced capital is not yet a yes."},"pay":{"concept":"Dark: the asking price for FSC never appears anywhere. The case delivers a verdict on an acquisition without once naming what is being paid for it.","from":"Never given, and never asked for. The prompt narrows the question to 'is there room to increase profitability', which quietly excuses the omission.","matters":"A 41.6% margin is worth a different amount at every price. The case answers 'can it be fixed' and then recommends 'buy it', which is a different question."},"profit":{"concept":"Margin rather than dollars — profit as a share of the $1,000. Because revenue is pinned at exactly $1,000, each margin here is just the cost figure read backwards.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed three times: at step 3 from the $900, at step 4 from the $650, at step 6 from the $584. The 40% pass mark is given only if the candidate asks.","computes":"($1,000 − $584) ÷ $1,000 = 41.6%, against 10% at the start and 35% after alignment.","mental":"Revenue is exactly $1,000, so the dollars left over ARE the margin: $416 means 41.6%. When the denominator is a power of ten, move the point and stop.","matters":"35% after negotiating every cost line; 41.6% after one change in how many people are on the ship. The ranking of those two moves is what the case exists to teach."}},"KEL-02":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to open an eleventh campus, in Chicago. The client already has the money set aside, so the question is not whether it can afford it but whether enough students would enrol.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The clarifying page sets the bar: positive operating profit for the new campus two years after opening.","matters":"The answer is no, and it is settled by comparing two student counts rather than two sums of money — 900 needed against 750 available."},"rev":{"concept":"Tuition, and nothing else — no housing, no supplies, no grants. One student enrolling for one school year brings in $15,000.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit A","from":"Derived at step 2 from Exhibit A, which reports the whole ten-campus company: $150M of revenue across 10,000 enrolments. Both are printed in thousands.","computes":"$150M ÷ 10,000 students = $15,000 a student.","mental":"Cancel the zeros before dividing: $150M has 8 digits, 10,000 has 4 zeros, so strike four off each and 15,000 is left. $15,000 a student.","check":"Exhibit A is the 2010 average of ten established campuses, and it is applied unchanged to a campus that does not exist yet.","matters":"Per-student is the only form in which this case can use revenue, because the unknown it is solving for is a number of students."},"cost":{"concept":"Two different animals held in one box. $6.3M a year arrives whether or not a single student enrols; $8,000 more is spent for each one who does.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit A","from":"Both backed out of Exhibit A's ten-campus totals. The $6.3M is the opex box below; the $8,000 a student never becomes a lump — it is netted against tuition instead.","computes":"Fixed: $48M ÷ 10 campuses = $4.8M, + $1.5M of amortised build = $6.3M a year. Variable: $80M ÷ 10,000 students = $8,000 each.","mental":"$48M / 10 just moves the point: $4.8M, + $1.5M = $6.3M. $80M / 10,000 cancels four zeros: $8,000. Every division here is zeros, not long division.","check":"Do not add the $4.5M build to the $6.3M. A third of that build already is the $1.5M sitting inside it.","matters":"The split is the case: the yearly lump sets what must be covered, the per-student figure sets what each enrolment contributes, and breakeven is one divided by the other."},"vol":{"concept":"Two student counts, and the whole case exists to compare them: the 900 enrolments needed to cover costs, and the 750 Chicago could realistically attract.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit B","from":"900 is computed at step 3. 750 arrives at step 4 from the 10% share Exhibit B shows for campuses that face a competitor, applied to the Chicago pool.","computes":"Needed: $6.3M ÷ $7,000 = 900. Available: 1,000 high schools × 6 prospects = 6,000; × 10% = 600 from school, ÷ 80% = 750 with adults.","mental":"$6.3M / $7,000: cancel three zeros, 6,300 / 7 = 900. Then dividing by 80% is multiplying by 5/4, so 600 becomes 750 - dividing by four-fifths makes a number bigger.","check":"The case prints '10% market share equals 750' and skips that last step. The 10% falls on the 6,000 school prospects; the extra 150 are the 20% adult share grossed up.","matters":"150 students short is the answer. Nothing else is ever compared — not profit, not payback — so this one gap is the no."},"price":{"concept":"Tuition for one year of the course: the same $15,000 the revenue box holds. In this case the rate and the revenue per unit are simply the same number.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit A","from":"Never stated as a price. It is the ten-campus average backed out of Exhibit A, so it is what CCU has charged elsewhere, not what Chicago's market would bear.","mental":"Take $1,000 off $7,000 and contribution is $6,000, six-sevenths. Breakeven moves the other way, x 7/6: 900 plus a sixth of 900 is 1,050. Reciprocals, so they never match.","check":"Ours: the recommendation floats scholarships to lift enrolment. That is a cut to this box, and the case never works out what it would do to the 900.","matters":"It sets the $7,000 each student contributes; a $1,000 discount moves breakeven from 900 to 1,050 and widens a gap that is already fatal."},"opex":{"concept":"What the campus owes each year whoever turns up: building and equipment, recruiting and admin — plus one year's slice of the Chicago renovation.","litAt":"step 2 · Exhibit A","from":"The $4.8M is Exhibit A's $48M of fixed costs split ten ways. The $1.5M is the $4.5M build spread over three years, given at step 3 only if the candidate asks.","computes":"$48M ÷ 10 campuses = $4.8M a year, + $4.5M ÷ 3 years = $1.5M a year, giving $6.3M.","mental":"$48M / 10 is a decimal-point shift, $4.8M. $4.5M / 3 is thirding 45, so $1.5M. Then 4.8 + 1.5 = 6.3, the tenths carrying into a whole.","matters":"This is the number divided by $7,000 to get the 900. Every $7,000 added to it is one more student Chicago has to find."},"capex":{"concept":"The one-off $4.5M to renovate the Chicago site. Unlike most cases it is never weighed on its own — it is cut into thirds and folded into the yearly costs.","litAt":"step 3 · Breakeven","from":"Given at step 3 only if the candidate asks about the investment, together with the instruction to amortise it evenly over three years.","computes":"$4.5M ÷ 3 years = $1.5M a year, which is the second term of the $6.3M.","mental":"$4.5M / 3 = $1.5M. To feel its weight, drop it: $4.8M / $7,000 is 4,800 / 7, near 690. The build is worth 210 students, close to a quarter of the 900.","check":"Three years of amortisation against a goal set two years after opening: year three still carries $1.5M of a building paid for in year one.","matters":"It is the only Chicago-specific cost in the case and nearly a quarter of what must be covered — drop it and breakeven falls from 900 to about 690."},"profit":{"concept":"What one extra student leaves behind after her own costs: $7,000. Not the campus's profit — the campus loses money until enough of these have piled up.","litAt":"step 3 · Breakeven","from":"Computed at step 3 from the two per-student figures. Exhibit A's own operating profit line, $22M across ten campuses, is never used for anything.","computes":"$15,000 tuition − $8,000 variable cost = $7,000 a student.","mental":"$15K - $8K = $7K. Then 750 x $7,000 is 75 x 7 = 525 with zeros 1 + 3 = 4, so $5.25M against $6.3M - short by about $1.05M.","check":"At 750 students Chicago earns 750 × $7,000 = $5.25M against $6.3M of fixed cost — a loss of about $1.05M in year one.","matters":"Per-student profit is the exchange rate of this case: it is what turns $6.3M of fixed cost into the 900 students that decide the answer."},"pay":{"concept":"No payback in years is ever computed. The shortfall stands in for it: how far Chicago lands below the enrolment that would cover its own investment.","litAt":"step 5 · The gap","from":"Computed at step 5 by subtraction. Both inputs were fixed earlier and neither is revisited.","computes":"900 needed − 750 expected = 150 students short.","mental":"900 - 750 = 150. Times $7,000: 15 x 7 = 105 with the zeros back, $1.05M. And 750/900 is 5/6, which is 83% - read the fraction instead of dividing.","check":"150 × $7,000 = $1.05M of missing contribution a year; Chicago reaches about 83% of the enrolment it needs.","matters":"The case closes on the harder version: the 10% share belongs to campuses open three years or more, so year-one Chicago should expect fewer than 750, not more."}},"KEL-14":{"decision":{"concept":"Revenue up, fixed costs flat, input prices unchanged — and profit down anyway. The only surviving explanation is that the sales mix changed, and that is the whole case.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, which quietly closes three branches before you start: revenue grew, fixed costs held, formulations and wastage unchanged.","matters":"Named correctly this is a pricing decision, not a cost one. The answer — Premium to $33 — sits in Exhibit C, which you only get to by ruling cost out first."},"rev":{"concept":"Cases of soup sold to grocery buyers across three lines. Revenue rose 14% in F15, and that rise is precisely what makes the profit fall confusing.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Exhibit A gives the yearly totals; Exhibit B gives the per-line prices and volumes that rebuild them exactly. Worth proving they agree before trusting Exhibit B.","computes":"F14: 80M × $28 + 60M × $24 = $2,240M + $1,440M = $3,680M, matching Exhibit A to the dollar.","mental":"8 × 28 = 224 and 6 × 24 = 144; 224 + 144 = 368, then restore the scale to $3,680M. Do the digits, add, then put the zeros back on once.","matters":"Revenue rising while profit falls is the case's puzzle. Proving the two exhibits reconcile is what licenses you to use Exhibit B's per-case margins later."},"vol":{"concept":"Cases of soup across Traditional, Light and Premium. Volume rose 15M cases in F15 — but 40M of it is a brand-new line while both old lines shrank.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Exhibit A's total row, broken into lines by Exhibit B. F14 had no Premium at all, so the two years are not the same portfolio being compared.","computes":"F15: Traditional 60M + Light 55M + Premium 40M = 155M. Against F14, Traditional fell 20M and Light fell 5M.","mental":"60 + 55 + 40: pair the 60 and 40 into 100 first, then add 55. Look for the pair that makes a round number before adding in the order written.","matters":"Those 20M and 5M lost cases are the cannibalisation. At $10 of margin each they cost $250M, which is how 40M cases of a new line made the company worse off."},"price":{"concept":"An average across three lines, and it rose — which rules out discounting as the cause. The individual line prices never moved: $28, $24 and $30 a case.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit B","from":"Printed nowhere. It has to be divided out of Exhibit A, which is exactly what the casebook means when it says revenue outpaced volume.","computes":"$3,680M ÷ 140.0M = $26.29 in F14; $4,200M ÷ 155.0M = $27.10 in F15.","mental":"140 × 26 = 3,640, leaving 40, and 40 is a bit under a third of 140 — so $26.29. Multiply up to get close, then read the remainder as a fraction of the divisor.","matters":"Average price up and profit down kills the obvious story. It forces the diagnosis onto mix, the one explanation that survives all three of the prompt's exclusions."},"cost":{"concept":"Everything that moves with a case of soup — broth, meat, vegetables, spices, packaging, wages. It is not a row on any exhibit: it is the gap between two rows that are.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Derived from Exhibit A. Contribution margin is revenue minus variable cost, so variable cost is revenue minus contribution margin.","computes":"F14: $3,680M − $1,360M = $2,320M. F15: $4,200M − $1,356M = $2,844M — up $524M on $520M more revenue.","mental":"4,200 − 1,356: take off 1,400 to get 2,800, then hand back the 44 you over-subtracted, giving 2,844. Round the subtrahend up, then correct.","matters":"Variable cost grew by more than revenue did. That single comparison is the diagnosis, and it can be made before any product-line exhibit is opened."},"opex":{"concept":"Everything that does not move with a case: facilities, R&D, marketing, distribution. Flat at $1,300M across F14 and F15, which removes it as a suspect for F15.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Never printed. It is the gap between contribution margin and gross margin on Exhibit A — the casebook writes this as 'GM − CM', which has the two terms the wrong way round.","computes":"F14: $1,360M − $60.0M = $1,300M. F15: $1,356M − $56.0M = $1,300M. Identical, to the million.","check":"F13's gap is $1,264M − $63.9M = $1,200M, so the fixed-cost spike the prompt blames for F14 was about $100M — real, and not repeated in F15.","mental":"1,360 − 60 = 1,300 and 1,356 − 56 = 1,300 as well: spot that both gaps are the same before doing either subtraction properly. F13's 1,264 − 64 = 1,200, a clean $100M step.","matters":"The prompt blames a fixed-cost spike for F14, and this shows the spike happened but did not recur. It closes the branch most candidates open first."},"capex":{"concept":"The box stays dark, and that is worth noticing. The case charges nothing at all for launching a whole new line in cartons rather than cans — no plant, no tooling, no launch marketing.","from":"Nothing, anywhere in the case. Fixed costs are flat at $1,300M across F14 and F15, so Exhibit A is asserting that the Premium launch cost nothing to build.","matters":"It flatters Premium. Even with a free launch the line destroyed $10M of profit; any real launch cost would only deepen that and strengthen the recommendation."},"profit":{"concept":"What is left once both kinds of cost are paid. It is tiny — $56M on $4,200M of revenue, about 1.3% — so a $4M swing is a fourteenth of the entire result.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"A printed row on Exhibit A. It falls in four of the five years shown while revenue rises in all five — the pattern that makes this a mix problem, not a one-year accident.","computes":"F15: $4,200M − $2,844M variable − $1,300M fixed = $56.0M, down $4.0M from F14.","check":"The fix — Premium at $33 — turns a −$10M line into +$160M, lifting gross margin to about $226M. Four times $56M is $224M, so 'more than quadruple' clears by $2M.","mental":"56 + 170 = 226 against 4 × 56 = 224. Compute the claim's threshold separately and compare, rather than dividing 226 by 56 and squinting at the answer.","matters":"On a 1.3% margin, mix is everything. A line earning $6 a case that destroys $250M of $10-a-case margin is not a rounding error — it is the whole result."}},"KEL-16":{"decision":{"concept":"How an investor would lift profit at a company whose sales are falling 8% a year. The prompt hands you the answer in one clause: three plants running at 50%.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The market facts at step 2 — a 5% annual decline, superstores taking share — are all outside the client's control and are meant to be set aside.","check":"Ours: keep the US plants instead — 8,500 × $7.9k + 4,000 × $8.5k + $15m + $18m = $134.2m, a saving under $2m. The Mexican option is worth about thirty times more.","mental":"Round and adjust: 8,500 × 8 = 68,000, then take back 8,500 × 0.1 = 850, giving 67,150. Multiply by the neighbouring whole number and correct once.","matters":"The recommendation is to close two factories and drop 500 product lines. Nothing in it touches the shrinking market, and the case never admits that."},"rev":{"concept":"Sales, and in this case the thing you give up. Moving everything into one plant means 500 product lines will not fit, and those lines were earning money.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt for 1991. The $264m is computed at step 6, after the 500 lines are dropped.","computes":"$275m ÷ 12,500 lines = $22,000 each, so 500 lines is $11m, about 4%. $275m − $11m = $264m.","mental":"500 out of 12,500 is one twenty-fifth. Dividing by 25 is dividing by 100 and multiplying by 4: $2.75m × 4 = $11m. Turn an awkward fraction into two easy steps.","matters":"It assumes every line earns the average. If the 500 dropped are the slowest sellers the loss is smaller; if they are why wholesalers buy from you at all, it is far larger."},"cost":{"concept":"Everything spent, and this case's one real idea: the part of it that stays put when output falls is what has been eating the profit.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Ours — no total cost appears anywhere. It is revenue minus pre-tax profit, and even the $25m profit it rests on is stated in a bracket at step 6.","computes":"$275m − $25m = $250m today; $264m − $82m = $182m after. Only $136m of the $250m is plant cost.","mental":"Take the round number off first: 264 − 82 is 264 − 80 = 184, then − 2 = 182. Split the subtrahend into a round part and a remainder.","matters":"The other $114m — marketing, five divisions' overheads, the 45% of costs that are shared — is never examined, and in a shrinking business that is where the next answer lives."},"vol":{"concept":"Product lines, not units. The axis says 'Units', the legend says SKU and step 6 says SLU — three names for one quantity, and that quantity is the denominator of every figure here.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit B","from":"Read off Exhibit B at step 4. The 12,000 is Chihuahua's full height, which is not printed — you have to add its used and unused bars together.","computes":"Used: 4,500 + 5,000 + 3,000 = 12,500. Chihuahua's capacity: 4,500 + 7,500 = 12,000.","mental":"Add the used bars in pairs — 4,500 + 5,000 = 9,500, plus 3,000 = 12,500. Landing exactly on the 12.5k SKUs from step 2 is the check, not a coincidence.","matters":"One plant holds 12,000 of the 12,500. That 500-line near-miss is the case telling you its answer, and it is the only reason any revenue has to be given up."},"price":{"concept":"Not a price anyone charges. It is revenue spread evenly over the catalogue, invented only so that the 500 discontinued lines can be given a value.","litAt":"step 6 · The maths","from":"Computed at step 6 as $275m ÷ 12,500. The case never checks whether lines earn anything like equally — its best seller is a single branded stapler.","computes":"$275m ÷ 12,500 lines = $22,000 a line a year.","mental":"Double both sides to make it friendly: 275 ÷ 12.5 is the same as 550 ÷ 25, and 25 × 22 = 550. Scaling top and bottom equally leaves a division unchanged.","matters":"Everything surrendered by this recommendation is priced with that one average. It is the softest number in the case and it is carrying $11m."},"opex":{"concept":"What the three factories cost to run in a year, split into the part that stays if you make nothing and the part that arrives per line. That split is the whole decision.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit B","from":"Read off Exhibit C at step 5 as six bars, fixed and variable per plant. The cost per line is computed in the same step; it is on no chart.","computes":"$38m + $54.5m + $43.5m = $136m. After: $20m fixed + ($4,000 × 12,000 lines) = $20m + $48m = $68m.","check":"Variable per line: $18m ÷ 4,500 = $4.0k in Mexico, $39.5m ÷ 5,000 = $7.9k in Michigan, $25.5m ÷ 3,000 = $8.5k in New Jersey.","mental":"Digits first, zeros last: 4 × 12 = 48, then three zeros plus three zeros, so $48,000,000. For $39.5m ÷ 5,000, halve and double — 39.5 ÷ 5 = 7.9.","matters":"Chihuahua looks worst on both charts — emptiest, and the largest fixed cost — and is the plant to keep. Only cost per line decides, and it is printed on neither exhibit."},"capex":{"concept":"Dark, and it should not be. Closing two factories costs money once — severance, write-offs, moving the work, a transition plan — and the case names none of it.","from":"Never given. Step 7 files unionised labour, warehousing and culture under 'qualitative issues', which is how a one-off cost gets discussed without a number.","matters":"A $57m-a-year gain against an unknown one-off is still almost certainly a yes, but with no figure there is no payback — and the client here is an investor."},"profit":{"concept":"Pre-tax profit, and the figure the entire recommendation is quoted against, though it is the one number in the case that appears on no exhibit.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Stated in a bracket at step 6: 'currently $25m/year'. Exhibit A's dotted line ends nearer $15m, and its −40% CAGR off the 1989 peak implies about $31m.","computes":"$25m + $68m of cost taken out − $11m of revenue given up = $82m, more than three times the starting figure.","mental":"Net the two changes against each other before touching the base: 68 − 11 = 57, then 57 + 25 = 82. One addition instead of two.","matters":"The $57m improvement is solid. The 'triples profit' headline is only as good as a $25m starting point that the exhibits do not support."}},"KEL-17":{"decision":{"concept":"Whether to pay $25m for a packaging maker that is currently losing money. The offer is final, so the usual lever — negotiate the price down — is shut before the case begins.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1, price included. Unusually, the asking price comes first, which is why the case can end in a valuation rather than in a diagnosis.","matters":"The answer is yes, and it is a yes about what the business could be worth rather than what it is worth. Exhibit C is the only box that actually decides it."},"rev":{"concept":"Sales, and they fall every year: $19.65m, $19.18m, $18.82m. The falling line is the visible symptom, and read on its own it points at exactly the wrong cause.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read off Exhibit A at step 3, handed over as soon as the candidate finishes laying out his structure.","mental":"Sales fall 0.83 on 19.65, under a twentieth, so about 4%. Units rise 3.45 on 32.75, roughly a tenth. Down 4 while up 10 can only mean price, and that is one division away.","check":"Down 4.2% over three years while units rose 10.5%. Both directions at once is only possible if each unit fetches less — and that takes one division to see.","matters":"Read as shrinking demand it suggests a market problem and a walk-away. Read against volume it is a pricing problem inside the company, which is fixable and therefore buyable."},"cost":{"concept":"Everything the company spends in a year, in three lines: making the goods, running the company, and writing down equipment bought two years ago.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Not printed. Exhibit A gives COGS, SG&A and depreciation as separate rows at step 3 and never totals them; the total is yours to add before anything else works.","computes":"$9.05m COGS + $7.50m SG&A + $3.45m depreciation = $20.00m, against $18.82m of sales.","mental":"Find the pair that rounds: 9.05 + 3.45 gives .05 and .45 making .50, so 12.50. Add 7.50 and it closes at exactly 20.00. Pair before you add, never read down the column.","matters":"It exceeds sales by $1.18m, and that is the loss. But only $16.55m of it is cash — the rest is depreciation, and no buyer of cash flows treats the two the same."},"vol":{"concept":"Units shipped, rising every year while sales fall. The sales force is paid on market share, so the thing that keeps growing is exactly the thing they are paid to grow.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Read off Exhibit A at step 3. The link to the pay scheme sits in the clarifying information at step 4 — the candidate must ask for it, or be steered to it.","mental":"3.45 more on 32.75: a tenth of 32.75 is 3.28, so a shade over 10%. Set that against sales down 4% and the roughly 14-point gap between them is the price cut.","check":"32.75m to 36.20m units, up 10.5%, while sales fell 4.2%. The gap between those two percentages is the price cut.","matters":"Rising volume is what rules out a demand problem and turns the case towards the sales force. Without it the falling revenue has no explanation but a shrinking market."},"price":{"concept":"Not a price the case ever prints. It is sales divided by units, and it is the single figure that explains everything else on the exhibit.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Nowhere. Exhibit A gives sales and volume in separate rows at step 3, and the casebook asserts the revenue drop 'is caused by a reduction in prices' without ever doing the division.","computes":"$19.65m ÷ 32.75m = 60.0¢; $19.18m ÷ 34.25m = 56.0¢; $18.82m ÷ 36.20m = 52.0¢ — exact to the cent in all three years.","mental":"Guess and multiply back rather than dividing long: is 19.65 over 32.75 near 0.6? 32.75 x 6 = 196.5, so yes, exactly 60 cents. The same trick lands 56 and 52.","matters":"8¢ off a 60¢ product is 13% of the price given away on rising volume. It causes the loss, and because a pay scheme causes it, a new owner can stop it."},"opex":{"concept":"The cash half of the cost box: making and selling. Kept apart from depreciation, which is a bookkeeping entry for equipment already bought and already paid for.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Ours, added from Exhibit A's COGS and SG&A rows at step 3. The case never groups them and never uses the cash / non-cash distinction at all.","computes":"$9.05m + $7.50m = $16.55m against $18.82m of sales, leaving $2.27m of cash generated in a year the company reports a loss.","mental":"9.05 + 7.50 = 16.55. Then count up instead of borrowing: 16.55 to 17.00 is 0.45, 17.00 to 18.82 is 1.82, so $2.27m of cash in a year the company reports as a loss.","check":"COGS per unit falls, 26.4¢ to 26.0¢ to 25.0¢. The factory is getting cheaper per unit, which is evidence against the case's own recommendation to cut moulds.","matters":"The company loses money on paper and still generates cash. For a buyer that is the difference between a rescue and a repricing, and the case never raises it."},"capex":{"concept":"The money the fund puts in — here the entire purchase price, not a building or a machine. $25m once, final, with no second round of negotiation on offer.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given in the prompt at step 1 and never revisited. What the fix itself would cost — new incentives, a smaller mould set — is never priced anywhere in the case.","matters":"Against Exhibit C it is the whole test: $25m is what the company would be worth once the margin is repaired, so the price already contains the improvement."},"profit":{"concept":"EBIT — what is left after every cost including depreciation. Negative two years running, and Plastic World is the only company on Exhibit B below the line.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Printed in Exhibit A at step 3. The −6% margin is not printed; the case asks the candidate to compute it, and it is the coordinate Exhibit C needs.","computes":"$18.824m − $20.00m = −$1.176m. Margin: −1,176,000 ÷ 18,824,000 = −6.2%, which the case prints as −6%.","mental":"1.176 on 18.824: 10% would be 1.88, so this is about two thirds of a tenth, call it 6%. Anchor on 10% and step down, and you rarely need the decimal at all.","check":"Add back the $3.45m of depreciation and 2018 EBITDA is +$2.27m. A case written for a buyer of cash flows never mentions this once.","matters":"−6% is the only use the case makes of this box: it is the coordinate you carry to Exhibit C. The swing from +$1.45m in 2016 is $2.63m in two years."},"pay":{"concept":"What the company is worth, read off a straight line against its profit margin. This case's substitute for payback: value is a function of one number, and that number is negative.","litAt":"step 6 · Exhibit C","from":"Exhibit C at step 6. The case reads two points off it — $25m at 0% and $40m at 'the industry average' — and never reads the point where Plastic World actually sits.","computes":"The line is $25m + $1.5m per margin point. At −6.2% that is about $16m today; at the competitors' 9.3% average, about $39m.","mental":"Six points below zero at $1.5m a point is 6 + 3 = $9m off, so $25m - $9m = $16m. Paying $25m for a $16m company is half as much again, a 56% premium.","check":"Ours: $25m asked for a $16m company is a 56% premium, paid up front for a turnaround the buyer must perform. The case calls 0% an encouraging milestone; it is break-even.","matters":"This is the box that should decide the case, and the case stops one step short of reading it. The recommendation is buy — a bet on the fix landing, not a bargain."}},"KEL-19":{"decision":{"concept":"Not 'is Salty Sole a good business' but 'can it be sold in 2012 for enough to give the fund a 20% return' — a question answered by working backwards from a sale price.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt. The 20% target and the roughly $300M sale value that embodies it are both handed over at step 4, not derived from anything.","check":"Exhibit A's own plan reaches $127.73M by 2012, a 2.7% annual return on the $111.64M the fund paid. Closing the gap to 20% is the entire case.","mental":"127.73 ÷ 111.64 is about 1.14, i.e. 14% in total over five years; 14 ÷ 5 is under 3% a year. Total growth divided by years is a fair first guess for small rates.","matters":"The answer is no under the existing plan, and the case shows exactly why: cost and price are both frozen, so only volume can move — and it cannot move far enough."},"rev":{"concept":"Net sales — gross sales less a discount that holds at exactly 1% in every one of the seven years. The company is planning modest recovery, not growth.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Exhibit A, printed rows. The casebook points out the discount line is flat, so nothing in the revenue story is hiding inside allowances.","computes":"2009E: $61.0M gross − $0.61M discount = $60.39M net. By 2012E: $70.0M − $0.7M = $69.3M.","mental":"1% is two decimal places: 61.0 becomes 0.61. A discount that lands on exactly 1% every single year is a sign it was assumed rather than observed — worth saying aloud.","matters":"Net sales growing 1.7% next year and 7.7% at best cannot produce a 20% return. Establishing that early is what pushes the whole case onto volume."},"vol":{"concept":"Pairs of shoes. It is the only variable left once price and both cost lines are declared immovable, so the entire case lands in this box.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Never printed. Volume has to be divided out of sales using the $25 average price given verbally at step 4 — Exhibit A is entirely in dollars.","computes":"$50M = $25v − (0.5 × $25 × v) − $15M → $65M = $12.5v → v = 5.2M pairs. Against 2012E's $70.0M ÷ $25 = 2.8M pairs.","check":"Ours: 5.2M pairs × $25 = $130M, against Exhibit B's 2009 market of $153M that is not projected to grow. That is 85% share, up from today's 40%.","mental":"$25 less half is $12.50 a pair; then 65 ÷ 12.5 doubles to 130 ÷ 25 = 5.2. And 130 out of 153 is near 130/150, six sevenths — about 85%.","matters":"5.2M against a 2012 plan of 2.8M is 86% more pairs, and more than double the 2008 and 2009 actuals. That comparison is the sentence the recommendation rests on."},"price":{"concept":"$25 a pair, and the case freezes it on purpose: industry research says adjusting it would not be prudent, so price is removed as a lever before you can reach for it.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Given verbally at step 4, printed on no exhibit. It is the figure that unlocks Exhibit A, since every row there is in dollars and not one is in pairs.","computes":"$60.39M ÷ $25 = 2.4M pairs in 2009E; $69.3M ÷ $25 = 2.8M by 2012E.","mental":"Dividing by 25 is dividing by 100 and multiplying by four: 60.39 → 0.604 → 2.4. Quartering the hundred beats dividing by 25 directly, every time.","matters":"With price frozen and both cost lines frozen, volume is all that is left — and the $25 is what converts the required $50M of EBITDA into a countable 5.2M pairs."},"cost":{"concept":"An identity, not a split into the two boxes below it. The $30.2M is COGS, the variable half; the $15M labelled 'Fixed Costs' is an annual operating cost, not a build.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Exhibit A prints all three rows and every column adds. The clarifying page explains why manufacturing sits in the fixed line: it is outsourced on a fixed contract.","computes":"2009E: $30.20M + $15.00M = $45.20M, which is 74.8% of the $60.39M of net sales.","mental":"The sum needs no trick; the ratio does. 45 is three quarters of 60, so 45.2 ÷ 60.4 is about 75%. Read the ratio rather than stopping at the total.","matters":"Both halves are declared immovable at step 2 — variable already at its market floor, fixed unchangeable by the company's own research. The cost branch closes in one page."},"opex":{"concept":"COGS: everything that scales with a pair sold — canvas, rubber, labour. Manufacturing is deliberately NOT here; the case contracted it into the fixed line instead.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Exhibit A prints COGS in dollars only. That it is exactly 50% of net sales in all seven columns has to be spotted, and the casebook expects the candidate to spot it.","computes":"$30.20M ÷ $60.39M = 50.0%. Also 24.75/49.5, 32.18/64.35 and 29.70/59.4 — exact in every year.","mental":"To test for a half, double the smaller number: 30.2 doubled is 60.4. Doubling beats dividing whenever you suspect a simple fraction is hiding in the row.","matters":"The flat 50% is what makes the backwards calculation possible at all: it turns the $25 price into a clean $12.50 of margin a pair so volume can be solved for."},"capex":{"concept":"The box holds the $15M fixed line, but this is not a build cost — it is outsourced manufacturing plus SG&A, paid every year. The real capital outlay was the $111.64M purchase.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Exhibit A, flat at $15.00M in all seven years. The purchase price sits in a different row, and that row is what the return is actually measured against.","computes":"$15M ÷ $60.39M = 24.8% of net sales in 2009E, falling to 21.6% by 2012 as sales grow — the casebook's 'about 23–25%'.","mental":"15 into 60 is a clean quarter, so start at 25%; the extra 0.39 of sales shaves it to 24.8%. Anchor on the clean fraction, then adjust for the bit left over.","matters":"Because it is flat in dollars, every extra pair sold drops $12.50 straight through to profit. That leverage is the only reason 5.2M pairs is arithmetically conceivable."},"profit":{"concept":"Earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation — a rough measure of cash profit. It matters here only because the sale price is a fixed multiple of it.","litAt":"step 3 · Exhibit A","from":"Exhibit A's own row. The $50M target comes from dividing the required $300M sale value by the 6.5× multiple and then rounding the answer up.","computes":"$60.39M − $45.20M = $15.20M in 2009E. Required: $300M ÷ 6.5 = $46.15M, rounded up to $50M.","check":"Ours: at the unrounded $46.15M the requirement is ($46.15M + $15M) ÷ $12.50 = 4.9M pairs, not 5.2M. The rounding quietly adds about 308,000 pairs to the target.","mental":"300 ÷ 6.5: double both to 600 ÷ 13, and 13 × 46 = 598, so 46.2. Doubling to clear a decimal divisor works here exactly as it did on the volume equation.","matters":"The casebook rounds $46.15M up to $50M for convenience and the required volume moves by 300,000 pairs. The verdict survives it; the number quoted to the client does not."},"pay":{"concept":"Not a payback period but an annual rate of return on the fund's own money, measured from what it paid to what it could sell for in 2012.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Exhibit A's last row prints 2.7% and nothing else. The base it is measured from is the $111.64M price row, which the exhibit never labels as the purchase price.","computes":"$127.73M ÷ $111.64M = 1.144 over five years, about 2.7% a year. Reaching 20% would need roughly $300M instead.","check":"Ours: the note says the acquisition was 31 December 2006, yet 2.7% only works from the 2007 price of $111.64M. 2006 EBITDA at 6.5× would imply $63.4M.","mental":"1.144 over five years: 14% in total is nowhere near a doubling, so per year it is roughly 14 ÷ 5, just under 3%. Spread small total growth evenly before reaching for compounding.","matters":"One row turns a company growing EBITDA every year into a failed investment. The business is fine; the price the fund paid is what puts 20% out of reach."}},"KEL-21":{"decision":{"concept":"EBITDA fell for three straight quarters while revenue jumped. The case is not asking whether profit fell; it is asking which of the two things behind revenue moved, because they have opposite cures.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt, along with the definition of EBITDA — earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation, which here just means profit.","matters":"Nothing in this case is arithmetically hard; there are simply five exhibits. The verdict — scale the menu back — rests on one division at step 5 and two survey charts."},"rev":{"concept":"Money through the tills. It rose 11% in the year profit stopped, which is the disguise: a rising top line is the symptom here, not a sign of health.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt for 2019; the whole 2017-19 series is read off Exhibit C's income statement at step 5.","computes":"$364m → $377m → $420m. The 2019 jump is $43m, about +11%, while EBITDA goes $170m → $169m.","mental":"Anchor on a tenth: 10% of $377m is $37.7m, and the rise is $43m, so a bit over a tenth. Percentage growth by anchoring, never by long division.","matters":"Revenue rising while profit stalls is what makes this a mix question. Had revenue fallen too, the case would have been about demand and the menu would never come up."},"cost":{"concept":"Here the cost box is really one line: food. Wages, SG&A and overhead barely move; COGS nearly doubles, and it does so in a year when orders fell.","litAt":"step 2 · Structure","from":"Read off Exhibit C at step 5. The casebook tells the interviewer to make sure the candidate notices COGS 'nearly doubled' rather than pointing at it.","computes":"$54m → $96m, +78%. Per order that is $54m ÷ 95m ≈ $0.57 of food in 2018 against $96m ÷ 80m = $1.20 in 2019.","mental":"Halve both sides to simplify: 96 over 80 becomes 48 over 40, then 24 over 20 — $1.20. Halving a fraction top and bottom never changes it.","matters":"The food in an average order more than doubled in cost. That is not inflation and not volume; it is a different recipe, which is why the fix is to change the menu back."},"vol":{"concept":"Orders, and the case's whole finding. Nobody counts them; they are recovered by dividing money by the average order, because revenue is orders times average order.","litAt":"step 5 · Exhibit C","from":"Computed at step 5 from two exhibits at once — revenue from Exhibit C's income statement, the average order read off Exhibit B's line chart.","computes":"$380m ÷ $4.00 = 95m orders in 2018; $420m ÷ $5.25 = 80m in 2019. Fifteen million orders gone, about a sixth of the business.","check":"On the printed figures, $377m ÷ $4.05 = 93.1m, so the true fall is 13.1m, not 15m. The casebook's 95m is about 2% high.","mental":"420 ÷ 5.25 is built to be clean: 5 × 80 = 400 and 0.25 × 80 = 20. Then watch the roundings — up on top, down on the bottom, both enlarge the answer, so they compound.","matters":"This is the case. Revenue rose because prices rose, not because more people came, and a price rise that costs you a sixth of your customers is the thing to reverse."},"price":{"concept":"Average money per order, not a menu price. It moves partly because prices rose and partly because people bought different things, so it is an outcome as much as a decision.","litAt":"step 4 · Exhibit B","from":"Read off Exhibit B, a line chart with no printed values — $4.05 and $5.25 come off the axis. The casebook then rounds $4.05 down to $4.00 to make its own division easy.","computes":"$4.05 → $5.25, a rise of $1.20, about +30%.","mental":"$1.20 on $4.05: call the base 4, and 1.2 over 4 is 3 over 10. Thirty per cent. Rounding the base down inflates it slightly, so say 'about'.","matters":"A 30% price rise against a 16% fall in orders still leaves revenue higher, which is exactly why the top line hid the problem for a full year."},"opex":{"concept":"Everything that is not food — wages, SG&A, fixed overhead. Its job in this case is to be ruled out: it moves $2m while the food line moves $42m.","litAt":"step 5 · Exhibit C","from":"Ours: the casebook never groups these three lines. They are $99m + $10m + $44m in 2018 and $101m + $10m + $44m in 2019, read off Exhibit C.","computes":"2019: $101m + $10m + $44m = $155m, against $153m in 2018.","mental":"Add the awkward pair first, then the round one: 101 + 44 = 145, plus 10 is 155. Both years differ only in the wage line, so subtract columns, not totals.","matters":"Wages rose $2m while orders fell 16% — the kitchen got slower, not busier. That rules out the usual cost-cutting answer and points back at the new menu."},"capex":{"concept":"Dark, and the darkness is the lesson. A new menu means new ingredients, new suppliers, new prep equipment; the case never puts a figure on what the change cost to make or to undo.","from":"Never given. The casebook's own risks page says scaling back could 'require asset divesture', which concedes there was an asset, and still prints no number.","matters":"The recommendation is to reverse a change whose cost nobody knows. Without it you cannot say whether reversing is cheaper than fixing the queue the new menu created."},"profit":{"concept":"EBITDA: profit before interest, tax and the accounting charges for wear and tear. Flat in dollars, which is the disguise — the margin underneath fell six points.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Read off Exhibit C at step 5; its quarterly shape comes off Exhibit A at step 3, where the EBITDA % row drops from 44-46 to 39-40.","computes":"$420m − ($96m + $101m + $10m + $44m) = $420m − $251m = $169m. All three years on the exhibit reconcile exactly.","mental":"Borrow to make round numbers: 96 + 44 = 140, plus 101 = 241, plus 10 = 251. Then 420 − 251 = 169 by taking 250 first and giving back 1.","matters":"Profit barely moved, so anyone watching dollars saw nothing at all. The margin row on Exhibit A — 46% down to 39% — is the only place the problem is visible."}},"KEL-24":{"decision":{"concept":"Which grape to plant on 12 acres the company already owns. Both wines use the whole plot, so this is a choice between two uses of one asset, not a question of how much to spend.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt as an open question about the land. The case narrows it to Merlot-or-Bordeaux at step 2, without the candidate having chosen that narrowing.","matters":"The answer reverses between step 2 and step 3: Bordeaux wins on annual profit, Merlot wins once the waiting is counted, and the recommendation is Merlot."},"rev":{"concept":"One year's sales of whichever wine is planted. The two options are built to land in exactly the same place here, so revenue is unable to decide anything.","litAt":"step 2 · Which wine","from":"Computed at step 2 from yields and prices given in the same breath; the case's own table prints both columns.","computes":"Merlot: 12,000 kg × 2 L/kg × $20 = $480,000. Bordeaux: 12,000 kg × 1 L/kg × $40 = $480,000.","mental":"Both are the same sum grouped differently: 2 litres at $20 and 1 litre at $40 are both $40 a kilo. 12 x 4 = 48, zeros 3 + 1 = 4, so $480,000 each.","matters":"Twice the litres at half the price lands on one number — a deliberate tie that pushes the decision down into margin, and then into timing."},"cost":{"concept":"No cost is ever named. Costs appear only inside the margins — 10% on Merlot, 15% on Bordeaux — so 90 cents of every Merlot dollar is a cost you never see itemised.","from":"The margins are given at step 2 when the candidate asks. Step 3 adds one rule: no cost is incurred until the wine is sold, six or twelve years out.","mental":"10% is a tenth - knock a digit off - so 90 cents in every Merlot dollar is cost you never see. 15% is 10% plus half of it again: anchor on the tenth and add the half.","check":"Ours: that rule means the vineyard is farmed and the wine stored for 6 or 12 years free of charge. The case lists inventory cost only as something a strong candidate mentions.","matters":"Revenue ties, so the whole step-2 answer comes out of two percentages handed over without any explanation of what is inside them."},"vol":{"concept":"The harvest: kilos of grapes off the land, fixed by the acreage and identical whichever wine is chosen. What differs is how many litres a kilo turns into.","litAt":"step 2 · Which wine","from":"Computed at step 2 from two given figures, 12 acres and 1,000 kg an acre; it is row C of the case's table.","computes":"12 acres × 1,000 kg = 12,000 kg; at 2 L/kg that is 24,000 L of Merlot, at 1 L/kg it is 12,000 L of Bordeaux.","mental":"12 x 1,000 = 12,000: the thousand is three zeros tacked on, not a multiplication. Then doubling for Merlot gives 24,000 L, while Bordeaux stays at 12,000.","matters":"It is the one quantity the client cannot change, so every later comparison is between two ways of converting the same 12,000 kg."},"price":{"concept":"What a litre fetches: $20 for Merlot, $40 for Bordeaux. Given, not researched — no demand, no competitor and no customer's willingness to pay stands behind either.","litAt":"step 2 · Which wine","from":"Given at step 2 alongside the yields. The case names 'customer reservation price' as something a strong candidate would question, which concedes the figures are unsupported.","matters":"The doubled price is exactly what cancels the halved yield. Move either number and the $480,000 tie breaks, and with it the reason the case turns on timing at all."},"profit":{"concept":"A year's profit once the wine is finally being sold — not a year from planting. For Merlot the first such year is year six; for Bordeaux it is year twelve.","litAt":"step 2 · Which wine","from":"Computed at step 2 as revenue times the given margin. The six- and twelve-year delays are withheld until step 3, after the candidate has already answered.","computes":"Merlot: $480,000 × 10% = $48,000. Bordeaux: $480,000 × 15% = $72,000.","mental":"10% of $480,000 is $48,000 - move the point one place. 15% is that plus half again: $48,000 + $24,000 = $72,000. Anchor on 10%; never multiply by 0.15.","matters":"On this box alone Bordeaux wins by half as much again, and a candidate who stops here gives the wrong answer. That reversal is the case."},"pay":{"concept":"What each wine is worth today, given that nothing arrives for years. Both streams are treated as running forever once they start — the vines never stop and are never replanted.","litAt":"step 3 · Timing","from":"Computed at step 3 once the 12% discount rate is given. Each perpetuity is valued in its own first year, then both are dragged back to year six to be compared.","computes":"Merlot: $48,000 ÷ 12% = $400,000 at year 6. Bordeaux: $72,000 ÷ 12% = $600,000 at year 12, halved back to $300,000 at year 6.","mental":"Divide by 0.12, never by 12: $48,000 / 0.12 = $400,000. Rule of 72 gives 6 years to double at 12%, so dragging Bordeaux back six years just halves $600,000 to $300,000.","check":"The halving is the rule of 72: 72 ÷ 12 = 6 years to double. Done exactly, $600,000 ÷ 1.12^6 = $304,000, so the shortcut costs $4,000 and changes nothing.","matters":"$400,000 against $300,000 overturns step 2: six extra years of waiting costs Bordeaux more than its better margin ever earns back."}},"KEL-27":{"decision":{"concept":"Buy one famous zebra. The prompt frames it sentimentally — a contribution to the animal community — and then says plainly, when asked, that the only question is profit.","litAt":"step 1 · Prompt","from":"Given in the prompt; the profitability goal comes out only in the clarifying answers, which also volunteer that zebras have a finite lifespan.","check":"Expected NPV straight off Exhibit A: −$1,195K + ($252K − $90K) ÷ 0.20 = −$385,000. The casebook computes this nowhere.","mental":"252 − 90 = 162. Divide by 0.20 by multiplying by 5: $810K. Against $1,195K paid today that is −$385K. Multiply by five; never divide by a fifth.","matters":"The answer is no, and the case reaches it by hedging — 'less likely that we can breakeven'. Nine times in ten it loses money, and on average it loses $385,000."},"rev":{"concept":"Ticket money. The zoo already takes $4.5M a year at the gate, and the zebra is being bought to add a slice on top of it.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 3 from 300,000 visitors × $15, and it matches the $4.5M the clarifying page quotes — so the two halves of the case agree with each other.","computes":"300,000 × $15 = $4,500,000 today. The lift at 8%: $4.5M × 0.08 = $360,000 a year.","mental":"8% is a tenth less a fifth of that tenth: 10% of $4.5M is $450K; take off $90K and you have $360K. Anchor on 10%, then adjust.","matters":"The lift is the only benefit anywhere in the model, and it is borrowed from a different zebra at a different zoo. Nothing else the framework names is ever quantified."},"cost":{"concept":"Two kinds of money that must not be mixed: three payments made today, and one bill that repeats every year the animal is alive.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Read out together at step 3, in a single list, with only one of the four marked annual. Sorting them into 'once' and 'every year' is the real work of that step.","computes":"$235K + $850K + $110K = $1,195,000 paid today. Then $90K a year thereafter.","mental":"Add the awkward pair first: 235 + 110 = 345, because 35 and 10 make a round 45. Then 850 + 345 = 1,195. Look for the pair that rounds before you start.","matters":"Put one line in the wrong column and the answer is out by a factor of five. Splitting one-off from recurring before computing anything is the whole lesson of the step."},"vol":{"concept":"Visitors through the gate. The zebra's entire case is that more of them come; nobody asks whether the ones already coming would pay more.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3. The 8% lift is a proxy — another zoo's experience with a similar zebra — and the case says so plainly rather than dressing it as a forecast.","computes":"300,000 × 8% = 24,000 extra visits a year, worth 24,000 × $15 = $360,000.","mental":"8% of 300,000: 8 × 3 = 24, then put the zeros back — 24,000. Digits first and zeros last keeps you off by a factor of ten far less often.","matters":"Merchandise is named in the zoo's own framework and never counted, so the lift is admissions only. That omission, unusually for this case, runs in the zoo's favour."},"price":{"concept":"One admission ticket, and it never changes anywhere in the case — no premium for the new exhibit, no discount to fill the gate.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3 alongside the visitor count. The framework lists 'change in ticket price' as a revenue lever and the analysis never touches it again.","matters":"Because price is frozen, every revenue figure here is really an attendance figure in disguise — which is why the breakeven can be restated as a percentage of visitors."},"opex":{"concept":"Feeding, treating and staffing one animal for as long as it lives. The only recurring cost in the entire model.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3. It is held flat forever — no inflation, and no rise in veterinary cost as the animal ages, which is the direction real ones go.","computes":"$360K of lift − $90K of upkeep = $270K a year of net cash.","mental":"Drop the thousands and subtract in the shared unit: 360 − 90 = 270, then restore the scale. Counting zeros mid-sum is where mistakes live.","check":"At a 7% lift instead of 8% the revenue is $315K, net cash is $225K, and $225K ÷ 0.20 = $1,125K against $1,195K paid — the deal is $70K under water.","matters":"Upkeep is a quarter of the lift. One percentage point of attendance is all the cushion there is, which is what makes the borrowed 8% so load-bearing."},"capex":{"concept":"Everything paid on day one: the animal, the habitat built for it, and shipping it from Africa. Two thirds of it is the building, not the zebra.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Given at step 3 and assumed paid today, so none of it is discounted. The $850K habitat is nearly four times the $235K price of the animal itself.","check":"At 20% this outlay costs $239,000 a year to service — $1,195K × 0.20 — which is what the breakeven revenue has to cover before upkeep is paid at all.","mental":"20% is a fifth. A fifth of 1,200 is 240; the base is $5K short of 1,200, so trim about $1K: $239K. Round to the friendly number, then correct.","matters":"It is the hurdle. $1.195M at 20% demands $239K a year forever, and the zebra's whole net contribution at the borrowed 8% is $270K."},"profit":{"concept":"What the zebra actually adds each year once it has been fed, treated and staffed. One flow, assumed identical every year, forever.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 3 from the $360K lift and the $90K of upkeep. Both are held constant in perpetuity, with no growth and no inflation on either side.","computes":"$360,000 − $90,000 = $270,000 a year.","mental":"Subtract in thousands — 360 − 90 = 270 — then sanity-check the story: the animal keeps three quarters of what it earns, which should feel generous, and does.","matters":"This is the stream the whole valuation prices. Divide it by the discount rate and you have the asset's worth; every remaining argument is about how big it really is."},"pay":{"concept":"Not a payback period — a net present value: what an endless $270K a year is worth today, less the $1.195M handed over now.","litAt":"step 2 · Framework","from":"Computed at step 4. Kellogg instructs that the flows run forever 'even though the Zebra will not live on to perpetuity', so the tail is never subtracted.","computes":"$270,000 ÷ 0.20 = $1,350,000, less $1,195,000 = +$155,000. Breakeven needs $329,000 of lift a year, which is 7.3% more visitors.","mental":"Divide by 0.20, never by 20 — dividing by a fifth multiplies by five, so $270K becomes $1,350K. Get it backwards and you are out by a hundred, with nothing later to catch it.","check":"Kellogg keeps the stream infinite where Booth's own zoo case subtracts the animal's death. On a 20-year life this NPV falls to about $120K; on ten years it turns negative.","matters":"The borrowed 8% sits 0.7 points above the 7.3% breakeven. Exhibit A then puts the expected lift at 5.6%, with only the 9% outcome clearing — probability 10%."}}};
